\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Workers to Get Additional Benefit From Minimum-Wage Hikes\b0\par\par\uc2 By Austen Hufford \par \uc2 764 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 January 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Low-paid workers might not notice that 22 states are increasing the minimum wage to start the year. The reason: Robust raises in recent years have rendered pay floors largely irrelevant, even in states that aggressively lifted them.\par \par \uc2 At the turn of each year, many states lift the minimum wage to adjust for the cost of living or to meet requirements in the law. Before the pandemic, that often meant a welcomed raise for low-wage workers, but labor shortages in recent years caused paychecks for many cooks, housekeepers and grocery clerks to rise faster than required by states. Meanwhile, the $7.25 an hour federal minimum wage, followed in 20 states, has been unchanged since 2009.\par \par \uc2 To start 2024, Washington state will raise the nation's highest minimum to $16.28 an hour, from $15.74 in 2023. Washington ties its minimum wage to the consumer-price index, a measure of inflation. Hawaii has the largest planned increase, with its minimum wage rising $2, to $14 an hour, in 2024. That is part of a law that will increase the lowest hourly wages in the Aloha State to $18 by 2028. California, New York and Illinois are also among the states raising the pay floor.\par \par \uc2 Most low-wage workers are making well above those minimums. Through September, the lowest 10% of workers by income in each state earned hourly wages that were on average nearly 50% higher than their state's minimum wages in 2023, according to a Wall Street Journal analysis that compared state minimum wages to income estimates from MIT Sloan School of Management professor Nathan Wilmers.\par \par \uc2 That is the largest gap between actual pay and minimum pay in a decade.\par \par \uc2 Because of inflation, Colorado's state minimum hourly wage is increasing to $14.42 at the start of 2024, up from $13.65 in 2023. Even with the raise, most low-wage Colorado workers will earn well more. The 10th-percentile worker in the state made an average of $16 an hour for the first nine months of 2023, up from $15.30 in 2022.\par \par \uc2 At a machine shop in Broomfield, Colo., the only employee getting a direct raise owing to the minimum wage increase will be the part-time high school intern.\par \par \uc2 "Our pay scale doesn't rely on minimum wage," said Andrew Igl, general manager of the machine shop, EPTAM Rapid Launch Center. "We plan on adjusting that intern wage by a dollar or so."\par \par \uc2 When David Aron started as a machinist in 2018, at a different shop, he earned the same $15 he received working at a local pizza joint. Now at EPTAM, he is making around $30 an hour. Aron attributes the growth to learning new skills, a strong work ethic and a shortage of wannabe machinists. He recently started saving to buy a home.\par \par \uc2 "There is pretty much always an opening," Aron said. "There have been midyear raises. They know the market is competitive."\par \par \uc2 Nationally, the 10% lowest-earning workers saw a 4.8% raise in the third quarter from a year earlier, according to the Labor Department. That is about half as strong as the prior year but still outpaces inflation and the increase in the minimum wage in the states that tie the pay floor to prices. Consumer prices in September rose 3.7% from the year earlier -- and inflation has cooled further since.\par \par \uc2 "Really tight labor markets are better for people at the bottom. When times are really good, they catch up," said \uc2 David Neumark\uc2 , an economics professor at the \uc2 University of California, Irvine\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The country is split between states with lower and higher minimum wages. Of the 30 states with a minimum above the federal level, most adjust the level annually, and 17 states mandate $12 or more an hour.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, inflation and wage increases have effectively erased the federal minimum. In 2022, only about one million workers made $7.25 or less an hour -- roughly 1% of the workforce, according to the Labor Department.\par \par \uc2 Some argue that pay rates for low-wage workers that outstrip minimums show the market can set fair wages without government intervention. Others say the recent bout of elevated inflation shows why pay floors need regular increases.\par \par \uc2 Over the past decade, unions and worker advocates successfully pushed for many states and cities to raise their minimum wages to $15 an hour. Now some want to go further, with at least one union pushing to double the former "Fight for $15" goal.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw480\pich486\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal7200\pichgoal7290
