\par\par \b \uc2 Business News: U.S. Ski Resorts Are Hotspots for Jobs --- Despite rate rises and global warming, employment tops prepandemic levels\b0\par\par\uc2 By Bob Fernandez \par \uc2 644 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 December 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par BF\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Warming temperatures and less-reliable snowfall hit ski slopes hard in some regions. Hundreds of runs closed, with abandoned lifts snaking through forests. But overall, Gen Xers, millennials and still-skiing younger baby boomers stormed the slopes after the pandemic lockdowns.\par \par \uc2 Add to that big snows in parts of the West, perhaps related to El Nino weather patterns, and cranked-up snow-making machines in the Northeast, and ski resort jobs have climbed to levels never seen before in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 Federal Reserve rate increases put a chill in the labor market. But U.S. ski resorts are hot pockets of employment, with monthly job numbers running 15% to 26% higher this year than in 2022, even in the summer, when resorts cater to families and warm-weather outdoorsy adventurers.\par \par \uc2 Arizona, New Mexico, southern Colorado and the Sierra Nevadas in California have been drier than in the past. But in northern Colorado, the Steamboat ski area last year got 428 inches of snowfall, or about 50% more than the average over the past decade, with almost 100 days of fresh snow.\par \par \uc2 "Going back to the '30s, if you have a lot of snow, you have a lot of skiers," said Michael Childers, associate professor at \uc2 Colorado State University\uc2  and author of "Colorado Powder Keg: Ski Resorts and the Environmental Movement." At some resorts, the ski season extends into April because of shifting weather patterns, Childers said.\par \par \uc2 Condos, second homes and ancillary development at ski resorts have "turned a lot of these areas into suburban sprawl," Childers said.\par \par \uc2 "The snow is late in coming in any real measure, but folks are still heading up every weekend" and facing long lift lines, he said.\par \par \uc2 Ski facilities are putting tons of money into new trails and higher-capacity lifts as well as financing some employee lodging in pricey resort towns. Companies recruit in South America for seasonal hires.\par \par \uc2 Alterra Mountain, which owns properties in the U.S. and Canada, said in March that it has put $1 billion into its facilities in recent years and expected to invest an additional $500 million in lifts, snow-making, summer adventures and infrastructure this year. Alterra Mountain owns facilities in Colorado, California, Vermont, West Virginia, Washington and Utah. Fifty million dollars in new employee-housing projects added 500 workforce-housing beds for the season, an Alterra Mountain representative said.\par \par \uc2 In mid-December, Aspen Mountain in Colorado opened 153 acres to skiers with a new lift higher up the mountain as part of a $13.8 million expansion, the first big one since the late '80s.\par \par \uc2 "The postpandemic boom in outdoor sports doesn't seem to be slowing down. We pretty much have our foot on the gas," said Aspen Mountain spokesman Jeff Hanle.\par \par \uc2 Millennials and Gen Xers have participated in greater numbers and "the younger cohort of baby boomers aren't exiting the sport as quickly as we thought," Melanie Mills, chief executive of Colorado Ski Country, the state's trade association, said earlier this year.\par \par \uc2 Ski jobs peak in the coldest months. In January, ski-resort employment hit 96,600, not seasonally adjusted, up 23% from the year-earlier 78,800, according to the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 . In January 2020, before the pandemic lockdown, the figure was 85,200. In October, the latest month for which data is available, ski resorts employed 29,400 people, up from 24,700 a year earlier.\par \par \uc2 The hospitality sector has been slow to recover from the pandemic, with jobs nearly flat compared with prepandemic times. Looking to tame inflation, the Fed cooled the economy with 11 rate increases. Monetary-policy actions, though, can neither change weather patterns nor dim the passion of skiers and snowboarders when it snows.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020231227ejcr0002w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
