\par\par \b \uc2 Wells Fargo Branch's Staff Vote to Unionize\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ben Eisen \par \uc2 631 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 December 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2  employees are forming the first union inside a major U.S. lender since the dawn of the modern megabank.\par \par \uc2 Eight employees at a branch in Albuquerque, N.M., voted Wednesday on whether to organize. Five voted in favor and three voted against, according to a tally by the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2 . The \uc2 NLRB\uc2  will finalize the results if no one objects to the election within five days.\par \par \uc2 The union will cover just a handful of the bank's 200,000-plus employees. But it amounts to a new frontier for the resurgent labor movement, following successful unionization efforts at \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  shops, a handful of \uc2 Apple\uc2  stores, and an \uc2 Amazon.com\uc2  warehouse.\par \par \uc2 It also will give momentum to the effort inside \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2 . \uc2 The Communications Workers of America\uc2  has been trying to organize there for the past few years, and hopes to expand its effort to other locations. (The union that represents some Wall Street Journal employees is affiliated with the \uc2 CWA\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 A branch in Bethel, Alaska, is set to hold a similar vote Thursday. Two more branches, in Daytona Beach, Fla., and Atwater, Calif., also have filed to hold elections. The union has been distributing literature to call center and branch employees.\par \par \uc2 "Our victory today is the first of many to come," Sabrina Perez, a banker at the Albuquerque branch, said in a statement. "Despite \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2 's aggressive attempts to dissuade us, we are igniting a fire and showing our colleagues across the industry that not only is change possible, it is within reach."\par \par \uc2 The union vote is just the first step. Workers still need to negotiate a collective bargaining agreement, a contract between the employees and company over the terms of their employment. That has proven difficult in other recent unionization efforts.\par \par \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2  branch workers have said they want to address issues around understaffing and compensation. Organizers also said they think the union can help repair the company's culture.\par \par \uc2 The bank is still recovering from a years-old sales scandal, in which management's unrealistic sales goals led employees to open millions of phony accounts.\par \par \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2  CEO \uc2 Charlie Scharf\uc2  was asked at a Senate hearing this month if he would remain neutral during the organizing effort. He said: "We believe that it's best that we have a direct relationship with those employees. We do intend to exercise our right to speak with them to make sure that they make an informed decision."\par \par \uc2 Sen. \uc2 Sherrod Brown\uc2 , who has previously supported the employees who are trying to unionize, expressed disappointment with that response. "It was an opportunity to show the American public truly a new day at \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2 ," the Ohio Democrat told Scharf. "I'm sorry that you failed to show that real change is afoot at your bank."\par \par \uc2 The bank has distributed fliers suggesting employees be skeptical about the union. "Don't be the union's bait," one says.\par \par \uc2 A spokeswoman for the bank said: "Employees should have all the facts and perspectives at their disposal on any important topic. That is why we are communicating with our colleagues -- just as the union has done."\par \par \uc2 The banking industry hasn't historically been a hotbed of unionization. There were some efforts after World War II and in the 1970s, but they didn't really stick -- similar to many other white-collar workplaces, according to Ileen DeVault, a professor of labor history at \uc2 Cornell University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "We firmly believe in the right for employees to have self-determination," Leach said in an interview. After workers voted to unionize, the employees and bank reached a collective bargaining agreement about 18 months later. The unionization effort hasn't changed day-to-day operations much, he said.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020231221ejcl0005m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
