\par\par \b \uc2 A Sector-by-Sector Breakdown of This Year's Union Wins\b0\par\par\uc2 1,038 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 December 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B6\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Auto workers\par \par \uc2 Union name: \uc2 United Auto Workers\par \par \uc2 Length of strike: 40+ days\par \par \uc2 Number of workers covered or affected: The \uc2 UAW\uc2  represents about 145,000 workers at \uc2 General Motors\uc2 , \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2  and Chrysler parent \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 . The union's targeted strikes hit nine U.S. factories and dozens of parts distribution centers, and they involved more than 45,000 workers.\par \par \uc2 Month tentative agreements reached: October\par \par \uc2 Key pay terms or increases: Auto workers won a 25% base pay increase over four years, plus the reinstatement of cost-of-living raises, improved retirement benefits, the right to strike over plant closures and better pay for temporary employees. The union didn't get a 40% raise or a 32-hour work week for a full 40 hours' worth of pay, both items that leaders initially put on the table.\par \par \uc2 AI and technology-related wins: The \uc2 UAW\uc2  fought to bring electric-vehicle battery production under its master agreement at the three automakers. It got partly there. The union convinced Ford and \uc2 GM\uc2  to put a few EV and battery plants under its main contract, pending a worker vote for some facilities. However, several key EV facilities were left hanging, and will likely be future organizing battle- grounds.\par \par \uc2 One surprising thing: The \uc2 UAW\uc2  called simultaneous strikes at all three Detroit automakers, talked openly about the back-and-forth at the bargaining table and took an unusually combative tone in its dealings with the company, regularly slamming the CEOs publicly. \uc2 UAW\uc2  President Shawn Fain's entire approach to talks was to keep the executives at \uc2 GM\uc2 , Ford and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  guessing -- and by extension, everyone else.\par \par \uc2 Las Vegas casino workers\par \par \uc2 Union name: The Culinary and Bartenders unions\par \par \uc2 Length of strike: 0 days\par \par \uc2 Number of workers covered or affected: 40,000\par \par \uc2 Month tentative agreements reached: November\par \par \uc2 Key pay terms or increases: Largest wage increase in the union's 88-year history\par \par \uc2 AI and technology-related wins: Casinos agreed to give workers advance notice and training when they introduce new technology that could affect jobs.\par \par \uc2 One surprising thing: The union forged the final deal with Wynn three hours before the strike deadline.\par \par \uc2 Hollywood writers\par \par \uc2 Union name: Writers Guild of America\par \par \uc2 Length of strike: 148 days\par \par \uc2 Number of workers covered or affected: 11,500\par \par \uc2 Month tentative agreements reached: September\par \par \uc2 Key pay terms or increases: Studios and streamers agreed to a series of increases in minimums: Most increased by 5% in September, will increase another 4% in the spring and again by 3.5% in May 2025.\par \par \uc2 AI and technology-related wins: Writers won't lose out on writing credits or compensation when AI tools are used to assist in creating scripts. Writers can use AI tools to write if the company agrees, but employers can't require the use of such tools.\par \par \uc2 One surprising thing: The union got studios to agree to a minimum number of writers for a show, an issue that studios and streamers initially opposed. Studios agreed to a lower minimum than the union initially sought.\par \par \uc2 Hollywood actors\par \par \uc2 Union name: \uc2 Screen Actors Guild-American Federation of Television and Radio Artists\par \par \uc2 Length of strike: 118 days\par \par \uc2 Number of workers covered or affected: 140,000\par \par \uc2 Month tentative agreements reached: November\par \par \uc2 Key pay terms or increases: Two wage increases in the inaugural year of the contract that amount to a first-year increase of 11.28%. An additional 3.5% increase in the second year.\par \par \uc2 AI and technology-related wins: Studios must get actors' permission to use their name or features in creating an artificial version of their likeness that includes one of their recognizable facial features such as their eyes or mouth. They will generally be paid when their likeness is used by generative AI systems and receive the same residuals they would for a physical performance.\par \par \uc2 One surprising thing: Creation of a bonus on top of increased residuals that funds a pool of money distributed by the union and studios both to actors in hit streaming shows and other union members working on shows that haven't been top performers. The union initially asked for a cut of subscriber revenue from Hollywood studios, but later revised how the bonus pool is funded.\par \par \uc2 Airline pilots\par \par \uc2 Union name: \uc2 Air Line Pilots Association\uc2  unions and the \uc2 Allied Pilots Association\par \par \uc2 Length of strike: No strike\par \par \uc2 Number of workers covered or affected: Over 45,000 pilots at \uc2 Delta Air Lines\uc2 , \uc2 United Airlines\uc2  and \uc2 American Airlines\par \par \uc2 Month tentative agreements reached: Delta's union leaders reached an agreement in principle in December 2022, and members ratified the deal in March. United's pilot union reached a deal in principle in July, and members approved it in September. Leaders of the union that represents American's pilots had a preliminary deal in May but had to negotiate some elements in response to United's. Members approved the contract in August.\par \par \uc2 Key pay terms or increases: Pilots won pay increases of 34% to 40% or more over the terms of the new contracts, as well as improvements in schedules, retirement benefits and vacation policies.\par \par \uc2 One surprising thing: The contracts included "snap up" provisions to ensure that another carrier didn't offer a better deal, bringing pay rates at the major airlines into alignment.\par \par \uc2 Delivery workers (UPS)\par \par \uc2 Union name: \uc2 International Brotherhood of Teamsters\par \par \uc2 Length of strike: No strike\par \par \uc2 Number of workers covered or affected: Roughly 330,000\par \par \uc2 Month tentative agreements reached: July\par \par \uc2 Key pay terms or increases: \uc2 UPS\uc2  pays new part-time workers a wage of $21 an hour. Existing \uc2 UPS\uc2  workers would get a raise of $7.50 an hour over the life of the five-year contract, including a $2.75-an-hour pay bump in 2023. \uc2 UPS\uc2  drivers will remain the highest-paid delivery drivers in the U.S. with the average top rate at $49 an hour.\par \par \uc2 AI and technology-related wins: Vehicles won't be equipped with inward-facing cameras. Driver-facing sensors will only be used for triggering in-cab audible alerts related to distracted driving.\par \par \uc2 One surprising thing: By the end of the new contract, the average full-time \uc2 Teamsters\uc2 -represented \uc2 UPS\uc2  driver would make $170,000 annually in pay and benefits, according to \uc2 UPS\uc2  Chief Executive \uc2 Carol Tome\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020231212ejcc0000i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
