\par\par \b \uc2 Washington Post Employees Strike For a Day After 1 1/2 Years of Talks\b0\par\par\uc2 By Alexandra Bruell \par \uc2 490 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 December 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Hundreds of \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  employees walked off the job for a one-day strike, an expression of long-simmering tensions over pay, workplace policies and planned job cuts.\par \par \uc2 The publication's union said it expected more than 750 staffers -- including newsroom employees -- to join Thursday's strike, which it said was the first of this scale since the 1970s. The Post newsroom is made up of more than 1,000 employees.\par \par \uc2 The union, represented by the NewsGuild, and management have been negotiating a new contract for a year and a half. The Guild said it is asking for pay increases of 4% for the next three years, and the company's latest offer was 2.25% next year, with 2% increases in 2025 and 2026.\par \par \uc2 "Post management has refused to bargain in good faith for a fair contract that keeps up with inflation and our competition," the union said. The Guild is also asking for a guarantee to maintain its current hybrid-work arrangement.\par \par \uc2 The company said it would "make sure our readers and customers are as unaffected as possible" by the strike, but didn't specify how it planned to ensure that news coverage would continue during the work stoppage. \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 , during a similar one-day strike a year ago, relied in part on editors with reporting backgrounds to cover news.\par \par \uc2 The Post has a sizable footprint outside the U.S., including teams of breaking-news reporters in London and Seoul who frequently cover U.S. news outside of traditional U.S. working hours. The Guild says it expects members abroad to strike in solidarity with U.S. colleagues.\par \par \uc2 The Post is dealing with declining traffic and subscriptions, following a surge during the Trump presidency and the start of the global pandemic. An effort to expand the Post's appeal beyond core political coverage has so far failed to yield desired results.\par \par \uc2 The company in October announced voluntary buyouts to reduce its head count by 240 and said it planned layoffs if the buyout goal isn't met. The reduction is expected to leave the newsroom with 940 journalists, the number at the end of 2021, editor \uc2 Sally Buzbee\uc2  has told staffers.\par \par \uc2 In an email to employees announcing the cuts, interim Chief Executive \uc2 Patty Stonesifer\uc2  said "prior projections for traffic, subscriptions and advertising growth for the past two years -- and into 2024 -- have been overly optimistic."\par \par \uc2 Jeff Bezos, the company's owner, recently told the newsroom that he wanted a return to profitability, according to a note to staff that was viewed by The Wall Street Journal.\par \par \uc2 Stonesifer, who assumed the interim CEO role after \uc2 Fred Ryan\uc2 's departure in June, will be succeeded early next year by \uc2 William Lewis\uc2 , a former CEO of Dow Jones and publisher of the Journal. Lewis is to assume the role Jan. 2.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020231208ejc800060\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
