\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Fresh Data Signal a Cooling Labor Market --- Available jobs declined in October to the lowest level since early 2021\b0\par\par\uc2 By Austen Hufford \par \uc2 591 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 December 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The hot labor market that underpinned a surprisingly strong economy this year is showing signs of cooling, an indication that growth could ease in 2024.\par \par \uc2 The number of available jobs at the end of October was the lowest since March 2021, the Labor Department said Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Fewer openings come as the unemployment rate has edged higher this year, Americans are taking longer to find new jobs, and wage growth is slowing.\par \par \uc2 The November jobs report, out Friday, could provide additional clues about whether the historically tight labor market is loosening further.\par \par \uc2 Job openings fell 617,000 in October to 8.7 million. That level is well down from a record high of 12 million in March 2022, though higher than before the pandemic began.\par \par \uc2 There are still plenty of jobs available -- more than the 6.5 million unemployed Americans seeking work -- but that gap has narrowed. The number of open jobs in insurance, real estate and retail have all declined during the past year. One ominous sign: Businesses have said they need fewer extra workers for holiday jobs this fall.\par \par \uc2 The jump in resignations earlier in the pandemic recovery has dissipated.\par \par \uc2 Economists see a declining rate of quitting as a sign that workers are less certain about the labor market or that they are more satisfied with their current roles.\par \par \uc2 In October, the quit rate held steady from the prior month at 2.3%, but has trended down since touching 3% in April 2022.\par \par \uc2 Workers might have good reason not to leave their jobs without another lined up.\par \par \uc2 The rate of hiring ticked lower in October from the prior month, extending a slow decline this year, suggesting employers are less desperate to fill roles.\par \par \uc2 Employers have added 239,000 jobs a month on average this year through October. That is a slowdown from nearly 400,000 a month in 2022 and more than 600,000 in 2021. Economists surveyed by The Wall Street Journal estimate that this cooling trend continued in November, with payrolls growing by 190,000. The Labor Department will release figures on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Hiring has eased this year as the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  pushed interest rates to a 22-year high this summer to combat inflation. That action has slowed the housing market and business investment, which has follow-on effects on the labor market.\par \par \uc2 There are exceptions. For instance, healthcare hiring is booming. The government and leisure and hospitality employers are also adding jobs at a solid clip.\par \par \uc2 When businesses were desperate to find employees in recent years, they offered workers some of the best raises of their careers. Now that companies appear less eager to hire, wage growth has cooled.\par \par \uc2 Pay gains remain above historic levels, and this year wages are rising faster than prices after falling behind earlier in the pandemic recovery. Slower wage growth can help cool inflation, especially for labor-intensive services.\par \par \uc2 More people are having trouble finding a job when they are laid off. Nearly two million Americans have been applying for continuing unemployment benefits in recent weeks, the most in about two years, according to the Labor Department. The level is still historically low and just approaching the prepandemic average.\par \par \uc2 Similarly, the unemployment rate remains very low. Economists estimate it held at 3.9% in November. But it has risen by a half-percentage point since the spring. Such an increase usually comes right before a recession.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw480\pich515\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal7200\pichgoal7725
