\par\par \b \uc2 Business News: Labor Pact to Cost Ford $8.8 Billion\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nora Eckert \par \uc2 606 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 December 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2 's new labor deal is expected to add $8.8 billion in cost over the roughly four-year contract, a hefty drag on the bottom line as the company pursues an expensive transition to electric, software-driven cars.\par \par \uc2 Ford on Thursday disclosed the labor-deal costs, along with re-establishing its profit forecast, after withdrawing it last month following a \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  six-week strike that culminated in the labor contract ratified this month. Ford said the strike drained about $1.7 billion from its bottom line, and it now expects full-year operating profit of $10 billion to $10.5 billion.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UAW\uc2  contract will add about $900 in cost per vehicle by the time it expires in 2028, an added expense that Ford will try to offset by reducing complexity in its lineup and using automation. The most costly terms included a 25% wage increase, a faster progression to the top wage for newer hires and new annual payments for inflation protections, the company said.\par \par \uc2 Chief Financial Officer \uc2 John Lawler\uc2  said executives assumed the added expense of the contract would be twice that of the previous agreement, in 2019, but it ended up being four times as costly.\par \par \uc2 "We have our work cut out for us," Lawler said during a \uc2 Barclays\uc2  investor conference on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 General Motors\uc2  on Wednesday said its \uc2 UAW\uc2  contract, along with a new labor deal for Canadian union workers, is expected to add $9.3 billion in costs, which was higher than analysts expected. The company outlined plans for a $10 billion share repurchase.\par \par \uc2 GM\uc2  said it was deferring some investment on electric and driverless cars and would cut spending on marketing and other areas.\par \par \uc2 During months of contentious talks with the companies, executives warned that the expense load from the \uc2 UAW\uc2  contract would exacerbate a competitive disadvantage to nonunion automakers in the U.S., such as \uc2 Tesla\uc2  and \uc2 Toyota Motor\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Those competitors are seeing wage inflation, though. Foreign-owned automakers such as \uc2 Toyota\uc2 , \uc2 Honda Motor\uc2  and \uc2 Subaru\uc2  said they would raise wages for workers to remain competitive with the market.\par \par \uc2 UAW\uc2  President Shawn Fain said the wage increases are a sign of the union's growing influence, and he urged workers to join its ranks. On Wednesday, the union formally launched organizing drives at more than a dozen nonunion automakers in the U.S. -- including \uc2 Tesla\uc2  -- one of its largest campaigns in history.\par \par \uc2 The Detroit companies' added labor expenses will hit as the strong pricing environment for new cars is expected to ease in 2024.\par \par \uc2 Ford and other automakers benefited from a sharp reduction in discounting and other incentives in recent years. Consumers on average paid $45,332 a vehicle in November, down about 1.9% from a year earlier but near a record high, according to research firm \uc2 J.D. Power\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Lawler said he expects pricing will come down further in 2024, as pent-up demand from the vehicle shortage that lingered in the wake of the pandemic dries up.\par \par \uc2 On electric vehicles, Lawler reiterated that prices fell much more sharply than the company had expected, but he doesn't expect EV prices to decline much further in the coming year.\par \par \uc2 Ford and other automakers have been pulling back on their investment in EVs, citing an easing in consumer demand. Lawler said Thursday that Ford would continue adjusting its spending based on the market.\par \par \uc2 "It's not about changing strategy. It's about changing the pace and flow of the capital and the capacity that we're putting in place," Lawler said.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw262\pich467\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3930\pichgoal7005
FF
