\par\par \uc2 The Property Report\par \b \uc2 U.S. Agencies Struggle to Fill Offices --- White House puts pressure to increase in-person work by federal employees\b0\par\par\uc2 By Peter Grant \par \uc2 834 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 November 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B6\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Biden administration is telling federal employees to get back in the office on a more regular basis. It isn't having much better luck than private companies.\par \par \uc2 The White House has been amping up the pressure on federal agencies to increase their return-to-office rate. White House chief of staff \uc2 Jeff Zients\uc2  sent an email in August to cabinet agencies instructing them to "aggressively execute" a shift to more in-person work in the fall. But administration officials say their return-to-office goals haven't been reached.\par \par \uc2 "We still have work to do," an administration spokesman said. "We're not there yet."\par \par \uc2 How far below the administration's goal is difficult to determine. It is up to individual agencies to publicize that data, the spokesman said.\par \par \uc2 But the \uc2 Government Accountability Office\uc2 's review of 24 federal agencies earlier this year wasn't encouraging. The GAO estimated 17 agencies had used on average only one-quarter or less of the capacity of their headquarters' buildings during a three-week sample period.\par \par \uc2 Federal agencies spend about $2 billion a year to operate federal office buildings "regardless of the buildings' utilization" and $5 billion annually to lease privately owned office space, the report said.\par \par \uc2 Other parts of the country with large federal workforces are struggling to bring back workers, said John Fish, chairman of the Real Estate Roundtable, a trade organization that has more than 250 member companies.\par \par \uc2 "Whether you're talking about downtown Boston, or Denver or Northern Virginia, occupancy is down substantially," he said.\par \par \uc2 Government return-to-office policies increasingly have been a hot-button issue for office and retail landlords and small businesses that depend on commuters for their livelihoods.\par \par \uc2 They have called for the public sector to make up for the anemic return-to-office by private-sector employers that stalled at a national average of about 50% of prepandemic usage earlier this year. It has been especially detrimental to the Washington, D.C., region, where about 270,000 workers are employed by federal agencies.\par \par \uc2 Office owners and brokers say they have seen a slight improvement in traffic in some federal buildings since Labor Day.\par \par \uc2 But the activity still falls far short of what is needed to restore the vibrancy of business districts, they say.\par \par \uc2 Many government workers are reluctant to return to the office for some of the same reasons that private-sector employees have stayed away, preferring to avoid long commutes or welcoming the conveniences of work from home.\par \par \uc2 The federal return-to-office policy is likely to become an issue in next year's elections. Republican members of Congress have introduced legislation to require federal workers to return to prepandemic schedules and have criticized the Biden administration for not doing more.\par \par \uc2 "It's not working," said Rep. \uc2 Clay Higgins\uc2  (R., La.) during a subcommittee hearing on federal workplace this fall. "We need our executive branch to perform in person in your office, end of story."\par \par \uc2 Some federal agencies are doing better than others. The Department of Veterans Affairs and the \uc2 U.S. Agency for International Development\uc2  reached their goals in adopting and enforcing new workplace requirements, according to the administration spokesman. Other agencies haven't reached their targets, the spokesman said.\par \par \uc2 The District of Columbia's office vacancy rate hit a record 20.3% in the third quarter, according to commercial real-estate services firm \uc2 Cushman & Wakefield\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Numerous federal agencies, including the \uc2 Securities and Exchange Commission\uc2  and the \uc2 U.S. Patent and Trademark Office\uc2 , shed space when leases matured, said Nathan Edwards, a senior research director at Cushman.\par \par \uc2 The federal government and some cities that are telling workers to come back are squaring off against unions, which stepped up the pressure to let employees work from home some days.\par \par \uc2 "Flexible scheduling is a viable option that does not hinder performances," the \uc2 National Treasury Employees Union\uc2  said following the release of chief of staff Zients' email in August.\par \par \uc2 Cities have done a better job than the federal government in bringing workers back to offices partly because mayors recognize the importance of commuters to property values and tax revenues.\par \par \uc2 In October, 71% of federal agencies offered hybrid workplaces compared with 59% for all government agencies, according to Scoop Technologies, a software firm that developed an index monitoring workplace strategies.\par \par \uc2 New York City Mayor Eric Adams called city workers back when he took office in 2022. But he agreed to a hybrid plan in labor talks negotiated with the city's largest municipal union earlier this year.\par \par \uc2 Mayors have had a harder time insisting that city employees have to come to offices five days a week when the federal government permits much more flexible schedules.\par \par \uc2 "When something like that happens at the federal level, it makes it difficult to do something different," said \uc2 Kathryn Wylde\uc2 , head of the business group Partnership for New York City.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw553\pich326\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal8295\pichgoal4890
