\par\par \uc2 REVIEW & OUTLOOK (Editorial)\par \b \uc2 A Labor Strike Over Shoplifting\b0\par\par\uc2 453 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 November 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Union walkouts these days are common for pay raises, but get this: Over the weekend hundreds of workers at three \uc2 Macy's\uc2  stores in the Seattle suburbs went on strike to protest rampant shoplifting and risks to employee safety.\par \par \uc2 United Food & Commercial Workers\uc2  Local 3000 says \uc2 Macy's\uc2  "is not doing enough to address shoplifting, violent shoppers, and other safety threats to workers and customers." Among other evidence, the union points to an incident involving Liisa Luick, a longtime employee at the \uc2 Macy's\uc2  store some 20 miles north of downtown.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Luick says workers at her store "frequently observe shoplifting and even occasional violence," and "the lack of security affects our customers too." But when she called 911 about "a repeat shoplifter that even law enforcement was familiar with," \uc2 Macy's\uc2  suspended her without pay "for nearly three weeks," she wrote in a letter to the Everett Herald last week. Now \uc2 Macy's\uc2  workers "are afraid to call the police because we worry we'll get in trouble or even lose our jobs," she said.\par \par \uc2 Macy's\uc2  declined to respond to Ms. Luick's claims but said "our top priority is to ensure the safety of our colleagues and customers in-store." \uc2 UFCW\uc2  Local 3000 said it filed an unfair labor practice charge against \uc2 Macy's\uc2  over its treatment of Ms. Luick and that the company "eventually provided back pay."\par \par \uc2 Ms. Luick isn't alone in worrying about crime. "Shoplifters and even violent customers are constant threats to our security," wrote Nicole Hardin, who has worked at the \uc2 Macy's\uc2  cosmetics counter for more than 15 years. Yasmina Grainat, another longtime employee, told the \uc2 Seattle Times\uc2  that the stress of dealing with thieves isn't worth her $20.73 hourly wage.\par \par \uc2 Macy's\uc2  and others can be forgiven for wondering if calling the cops is pointless. Theft up to $750 is a mere misdemeanor in the state, and many shoplifting cases are never prosecuted. Where crime is tolerated it proliferates.\par \par \uc2 A Forbes Advisor report last week ranked Washington as the worst state in the nation for retail theft: It "accounts for 48% more retail theft than expected based on its share of the U.S. population." Last year Seattle ranked fifth among cities and metropolitan areas most affected by organized retail theft, according to the \uc2 National Retail Federation\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 UFCW\uc2  3000 ended its strike Sunday night, saying "our hope is that \uc2 Macy's\uc2  will come to the table, ready to engage in good faith over solutions on safety" and other contract issues. The union could do more to ensure the safety of its members by exerting political pressure on Washington lawmakers to lower the felony threshold for shoplifting.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020231128ejbs0003m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
