\par\par \b \uc2 EXCHANGE --- Ford, \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  Workers Back Labor Agreements --- \uc2 UAW\uc2  members joined those at \uc2 GM\uc2  in ratifying contracts with 25% pay raise\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ryan Felton and Nora Eckert \par \uc2 749 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 November 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B9\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 United Auto Workers union\uc2  has secured worker backing for new labor contracts at all three Detroit automakers, bringing a tense round of negotiations between the two sides one step closer to wrapping up.\par \par \uc2 While the \uc2 UAW\uc2  has yet to officially release results, preliminary figures Friday show that a majority of workers at both \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2  and Chrysler-parent \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  have voted in favor of the proposed agreements.\par \par \uc2 At \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , the deal has been supported by about 68% of workers who have voted so far, with only a handful of smaller facilities having yet to post results, according to preliminary results published online by the \uc2 UAW\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Ford voters have so far supported the deal by the same 68% margin.\par \par \uc2 Votes from the remaining local chapters at both companies wouldn't be enough to overcome the current margin, according to the union's tally.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UAW\uc2 , Ford and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 The new 4 1/2-year pact covers about 43,000 \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  workers and roughly 57,000 union-represented employees at Ford. It includes a 25% base wage increase over the life of the contract, the return of cost-of-living adjustments and, for the first time, the right to strike over plant closures.\par \par \uc2 The close of voting will mark a major milestone for the \uc2 UAW\uc2 , after contentious negotiations that began earlier this year led to a roughly six-week strike. Workers at \uc2 General Motors\uc2  approved the pact by a slimmer margin earlier this week, according to preliminary results posted by the union.\par \par \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , led by Chief Executive \uc2 Carlos Tavares\uc2 , had been seen as a possible wild card for the \uc2 UAW\uc2  in this round of bargaining. The CEO, a hawk on costs who has won concessions from labor unions in Europe, led the company's first-ever \uc2 UAW\uc2  negotiations, after it was formed from the 2021 merger between \uc2 Fiat Chrysler Automobiles\uc2  and \uc2 PSA Group\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The automaker also has a broader global footprint and is less reliant on the U.S. than \uc2 GM\uc2  and Ford, although North America is an important profit driver. \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  owns the Ram pickup truck and \uc2 Jeep\uc2  SUV brands, both big moneymakers in the U.S. \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  has warned that significantly higher labor costs could make it harder to compete with nonunionized rivals. Tavares has worked to contain costs across the business, citing pressures of electrifying its vehicles and current market conditions.\par \par \uc2 This past week, \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  offered buyouts to roughly half of its U.S. salaried workforce, calling it necessary to protect the company's operations.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UAW\uc2  began talks this summer with Detroit's carmakers over new labor contracts covering about 146,000 workers. In mid-September, the \uc2 UAW\uc2  launched a simultaneous strike against all three companies, the first time in history, before reaching tentative accords with them last month. Thousands of workers at assembly plants and parts-distribution centers around the country were on the picket line as part of the \uc2 UAW\uc2 's strike strategy.\par \par \uc2 For Ford, this was the company's first nationwide \uc2 UAW\uc2  strike in more than 40 years.\par \par \uc2 The Dearborn, Mich., automaker was the first to reach a tentative deal with the \uc2 UAW\uc2  last month, which industry observers expected given its historically positive relationship with the union.\par \par \uc2 Still, this round of talks was more bitter than many company executives expected. \uc2 UAW\uc2  President Shawn Fain took personal digs at Ford's executive chair and CEO, spotlighting their compensation and the company's strong run of profits.\par \par \uc2 Ford Chair Bill Ford gave a rare address before talks concluded, saying the \uc2 UAW\uc2  and Ford should work together to compete against the foreign automakers and \uc2 Tesla\uc2 . Fain responded by saying that the union and Ford are no longer on the same team.\par \par \uc2 As part of the new labor deal, the \uc2 UAW\uc2  secured the reopening of a 1,350-employee Jeep assembly plant in Belvidere, Ill., which had been an important issue for \uc2 UAW\uc2  leaders heading into talks. The plant, which \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  idled indefinitely earlier this year, will make midsize trucks, and an adjoining battery facility will be constructed there, the union has said.\par \par \uc2 Fain and his team have begun working to expand the \uc2 UAW\uc2 's presence into nonunionized auto factories around the U.S. The union has launched organization drives at \uc2 Honda Motor\uc2  and \uc2 Subaru\uc2  plants, and electric-vehicle market leader \uc2 Tesla\uc2  is seen as a potential target for a campaign.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020231118ejbi0002k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
