\par\par \b \uc2 Business News: Starbucks Baristas Strike on 'Red Cup Day' --- Labor negotiations haven't progressed much since last year's walkout\b0\par\par\uc2 By Alyssa Lukpat \par \uc2 568 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 November 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Thousands of unionized \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  workers went on strike Thursday on a busy sales day for the coffee chain in a bid to bring the company back to the negotiating table.\par \par \uc2 Baristas walked out on the company's annual "Red Cup Day" promotion for the second straight year, according to the union representing them. During the promotion, \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  gives customers a free reusable cup when they order a holiday drink.\par \par \uc2 The union, Starbucks Workers United, said it wanted the company to bargain over pay raises, increased staffing and other concerns. The first \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  baristas voted to unionize in 2021 after that year's red cup promotion.\par \par \uc2 The union and \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  offered differing estimates of how many stores were included in the strike. The company said a few dozen stores were affected but most remained open. A union spokesman said around 5,000 workers at roughly 200 stores were expected to be on strike Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Union workers said on social media that baristas were on strike in Cleveland; New York City; Philadelphia, Richmond, Va.; and Washington, D.C.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks said Thursday morning that it was aware of the move by the union and was ready to negotiate. Starbucks Workers United said the strike was its biggest ever and asked customers to support them.\par \par \uc2 Employees on strike are calling the move a "red cup rebellion" on what they say is one of the company's busiest sales days.\par \par \uc2 In lower Manhattan Thursday morning, \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  baristas and a handful of supporters standing outside a cafe on Astor Place booed customers who walked inside. "Grinch!" one protester yelled at a man crossing the picket line. Other customers decided not to enter after seeing the protesters.\par \par \uc2 Workers outside the cafe said chants including, "\uc2 Starbucks\uc2 , \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 , you should know union busting's got to go." The store had a small group of customers inside, where it was difficult to hear the chants outside over the music playing on the speakers.\par \par \uc2 Mary Baca, a 22-year-old barista on strike, said the Astor Place store was staffed Thursday with managers and workers from other locations. She wants the company to offer more consistent schedules and higher pay.\par \par \uc2 She said she hoped the strike would persuade customers to support the workers who serve them every day. "We love our customers and hope they respect us," she said. "At the end of the day they're paying $7 for not-so-good coffee."\par \par \uc2 Workers at about 360 of the company's roughly 9,000 U.S. company-owned stores have voted to unionize. Unionized baristas are a small portion of the company's workforce but have gained traction since 2021, when workers in Buffalo, N.Y., voted to form the first labor union at a \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  cafe.\par \par \uc2 Negotiations haven't progressed much since last year's "Red Cup Day" walkout, when workers went on strike at more than 100 stores. Starbucks Workers United has said company representatives had walked out of negotiations when they couldn't agree on the bargaining format.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 , the world's biggest coffee chain by sales, has said it prefers to maintain a direct relationship with workers instead of working through a labor union. Workers have said a union is the best way for them to obtain better compensation and treatment.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020231117ejbh00021\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
