\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Holiday Hiring Demand Drops Off\b0\par\par\uc2 By Austen Hufford \par \uc2 544 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 November 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Businesses need fewer extra workers for holiday jobs this fall after fighting in recent years to find enough staff to stock shelves, fill boxes and deliver packages during the year-end rush.\par \par \uc2 That could be a warning of a weakening labor market, which is already showing signs of cooling.\par \par \uc2 The number of seasonal positions publicly advertised this fall fell to the lowest level in a decade, according to outplacement-services firm \uc2 Challenger, Gray & Christmas\uc2 . \uc2 The National Retail Federation\uc2  estimates that between 345,000 and 445,000 seasonal workers will be hired this year, down as much as 40% from a recent high in 2021.\par \par \uc2 Shipping company XPO said it expects its head count to stay flat in the last three months of the year. The \uc2 U.S. Postal Service\uc2  said it would hire about one-third of the seasonal workers added last year. \uc2 Macy's\uc2  announced it was seeking about 3,000 fewer seasonal workers than in 2022. Target and \uc2 United Parcel Service\uc2  are expected to bring on a similar-size extra staff as last year.\par \par \uc2 "The peak season holiday hiring is nothing like it had been in previous years," said Brian Devine, chief executive of Columbus, Ohio-based warehouse-staffing agency Ignite Industrial Professionals.\par \par \uc2 Many economists project that consumers will pull back on spending after a summer splurge that propelled blockbuster economic growth. \uc2 The National Retail Federation\uc2  expects spending this holiday season, which it defines as Nov. 1 through Dec. 31, to grow 3% to 4%. That would be down from a 5.4% increase in holiday sales last year and a 12.7% jump in 2021, according to Commerce Department figures.\par \par \uc2 Retailers and logistics companies have caught up on pandemic staffing shortfalls and can meet additional demand by having current part-time employees work more hours.\par \par \uc2 The seasonal hiring slowdown comes alongside other signs the labor market is downshifting. Overall job growth in October was half the prior month's gain, and the unemployment rate has risen a half percentage point since touching a multidecade low in April.\par \par \uc2 Retailers shed more than two million jobs in early 2020 as the pandemic took hold, and they struggled for years to staff back up, especially for the holidays when permanent jobs were in ample supply and employers were raising wages to attract workers.\par \par \uc2 Logistics and warehouse operators conversely added staff in 2020 and 2021 when consumers shifted to e-commerce and have recently significantly slowed hiring.\par \par \uc2 Warehouses and transportation companies employed 25,000 fewer people in October than a year earlier, after adding more than 400,000 workers over the prior year, according to the Labor Department. Retailers added 60,000 workers in October from a year earlier, a smaller annual increase than in the two previous years in the month when seasonal hiring starts to pick up.\par \par \uc2 In recent holiday seasons, flower and gift seller \uc2 1-800-Flowers.com\uc2  ended up with about 2,000 unfilled positions. It had to make that up with extra overtime for its existing workers. This year, finding workers has been much easier.\par \par \uc2 "We're really not having a problem with labor anywhere in the country," said Chief Executive \uc2 James McCann\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Liz Young contributed to this article.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw380\pich571\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal5700\pichgoal8565
