\par\par \b \uc2 Business News: Las Vegas Strip Workers Reach Deal With \uc2 MGM Resorts\b0\par\par\uc2 By Dean Seal \par \uc2 208 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 November 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Unions representing Las Vegas Strip workers struck a tentative agreement with \uc2 MGM Resorts International\uc2 , the second of three casino giants to reach a deal ahead of a potential strike that had been slated to start Friday.\par \par \uc2 The Culinary and Bartenders unions said on Thursday that with less than 24 hours before the strike deadline, \uc2 MGM\uc2  tentatively agreed to a new five-year contract covering about 25,400 workers at eight Las Vegas properties.\par \par \uc2 The unions reached a deal with \uc2 Caesars Entertainment\uc2  Wednesday and are still in negotiations with \uc2 Wynn Resorts\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Terms weren't disclosed. A representative for the Culinary union said a statement would follow later on Thursday. A representative for \uc2 MGM Resorts\uc2  didn't immediately respond to a request to comment.\par \par \uc2 The unions, which negotiate together, have said they are negotiating for contracts that would include "the largest wage increases ever negotiated in the history of the Culinary Union."\par \par \uc2 Union members voted in September to authorize a potential strike for tens of thousands of Las Vegas housekeepers, bartenders and other workers. Last week, the unions set a strike deadline for 5 a.m. Eastern on Friday if deals with the casino companies aren't reached.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020231110ejba00015\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
