\par\par \b \uc2 Hollywood Actors Reach Pact To End Monthslong Walkout\b0\par\par\uc2 By Joe Flint and Sarah Krouse \par \uc2 791 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 November 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Striking Hollywood actors reached a tentative agreement with major studios and streamers Wednesday that could clear the way for the entertainment industry to restart its content factory after nearly six months of labor strife.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Screen Actors Guild-American Federation of Television and Radio Artists\uc2  said its TV and theatrical committee approved a tentative agreement with the \uc2 Alliance of Motion Picture and Television Producers\uc2  in a unanimous vote Wednesday afternoon. The strike was set to end at 12:01 a.m. Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The end of the 118-day standoff means work can restart on new TV shows and films, restocking the pipeline of new fare headed to streaming platforms and the big screen. Actors can return to promoting their coming projects online and on the red carpet, bringing Hollywood glitz back to premieres.\par \par \uc2 The agreement follows weeks of tense negotiations between the union and the coalition representing studios and streamers such as \uc2 Netflix\uc2 , \uc2 Disney\uc2 , \uc2 Warner Bros. Discovery\uc2  and \uc2 Amazon.com\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The tentative deal "represents a new paradigm," the coalition of studios and streamers said Wednesday. It includes the largest increase in minimum wages in 40 years, a new residual for streaming programs, and "extensive consent and compensation protections" from the use of artificial intelligence, the \uc2 AMPTP\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 Terms of the actors' agreement weren't immediately available, although the union said the contract is valued at more than $1 billion and includes the creation of a "streaming participation bonus" as well as other wins such as "outsize compensation increases for background performers."\par \par \uc2 SAG\uc2 's national board is scheduled to review the tentative deal on Friday, and the union said additional details will be released following that meeting.\par \par \uc2 "We have arrived at a contract that will enable \uc2 SAG-AFTRA\uc2  members from every category to build sustainable careers," the union told members Wednesday evening.\par \par \uc2 Television and movie writers, who began picketing in May, ended their own strike against entertainment industry giants in September.\par \par \uc2 The dual strikes halted production of films and TV shows such as "Stranger Things" and "Abbott Elementary," disrupting fall TV schedules and postponing planned releases of major motion pictures from \uc2 Warner Bros.\uc2 ' "Dune: Part Two" to the next installment of "Mission: Impossible" from Paramount.\par \par \uc2 The strikes meant months without work for many of the crews, costume designers, hair stylists and others involved in productions and took a toll on the Los Angeles economy. Other states such as Georgia and New Mexico that are home to major productions also felt the pinch.\par \par \uc2 The Hollywood labor disputes were the culmination of years of frustration among writers and actors about the direction of the entertainment business amid a frenetic transition to streaming. That shift created new opportunities for talent and offered plenty of artistic freedom, but for many didn't provide the same financial security as the traditional TV and movie businesses. Eventually, the workers decided Hollywood needed new labor rules to go with its new economics.\par \par \uc2 Among the actors' demands were higher pay, more financial upside from shows and films that are successful on streaming platforms and protections from the use of artificial intelligence in the creative process.\par \par \uc2 As the labor dispute dragged on, movie releases including \uc2 Sony Pictures\uc2 ' "Kraven the Hunter" were pushed from a 2023 release to next year. \uc2 Disney\uc2 's much-anticipated new take on Snow White has been bumped to 2025 from next year.\par \par \uc2 In television, the broadcast networks have been relying on unscripted fare and acquisitions from overseas to fill the void left by the strike. \uc2 CBS\uc2  started to air old episodes of the Paramount Network hit "Yellowstone."\par \par \uc2 The end of the strikes ushers in a new era for studios and streamers that were already ordering fewer TV shows and films. Many streamers are struggling with profitability and industry executives expect consolidation of studios in the coming years as well as continued price increases for subscribers.\par \par \uc2 When the writers union resolved its strike, there was broad optimism in the industry that a deal between actors and studios would be close behind.\par \par \uc2 Negotiations between the \uc2 AMPTP\uc2  and the actors resumed in late September but fell apart soon after. The union's demand for a share of streaming revenue for a bonus pool led to a stalemate with studios and the alliance walked away.\par \par \uc2 Some high-profile \uc2 SAG\uc2  members, including George Clooney, Ben Affleck and Emma Stone, have pressured union leaders in recent weeks to come to an agreement with the alliance that would let the industry return to work. Both sides resumed talks later in October.\par \par \uc2 The producers alliance previously indicated that if an agreement wasn't reached by the end of October, there would be little chance of salvaging the 2023-24 TV season.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020231109ejb900024\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
