\par\par \b \uc2 Las Vegas Strip Workers Reach Deal With Caesars\b0\par\par\uc2 By Dean Seal \par \uc2 212 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 November 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Unions representing Las Vegas Strip workers struck a tentative deal with \uc2 Caesars Entertainment\uc2 , one of three casino companies working to avert a strike that could start on Friday if more agreements aren't reached.\par \par \uc2 The Culinary and Bartenders unions said on Wednesday that after 20 consecutive hours of negotiations, Caesars tentatively agreed to a new five-year contract covering about 10,000 workers at nine Las Vegas properties.\par \par \uc2 Terms of the agreement weren't disclosed.\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson for the Culinary union said a statement would follow later Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson for Caesars didn't immediately respond to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 The unions are still in talks with \uc2 MGM Resorts\uc2  and \uc2 Wynn Resorts\uc2  for new contracts that would include "the largest wage increases ever negotiated in the history of the Culinary Union," according to a previous statement from the group.\par \par \uc2 The Culinary and Bartenders unions negotiate together.\par \par \uc2 Union members voted in September to authorize a possible strike for tens of thousands of Las Vegas housekeepers, bartenders and other workers if a deal isn't reached.\par \par \uc2 Last week, the unions set a strike deadline for 5 a.m. ET on Friday.\par \par \uc2 The walkout would include about 35,000 workers in Las Vegas.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020231109ejb900017\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
