\par\par \uc2 PERSONAL JOURNAL\par \b \uc2 Should Employees Buy Disability Insurance Through Their Work?\b0\par\par\uc2 By Julia Carpenter \par \uc2 1,018 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 November 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A12\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Don't let the complexity of disability insurance keep you from buying it.\par \par \uc2 Disability insurance involves confusing paperwork and more than a little bit of math. It is designed to soften the financial fallout for employees who can't work for some time as a result of illness, injury or another qualifying condition. For a regular fee, you get income back for a predetermined amount of time.\par \par \uc2 But understanding how it works is only part of the equation. You also have to understand the cost.\par \par \uc2 Financial advisers say a typical policy bought through your employee benefits costs between 1% and 3% of your regular income.\par \par \uc2 If the policy being offered through your employer is higher than that, it may not be worth it, they say.\par \par \uc2 If your policy price is in that range, it's worth a real examination of the risks associated with your job and your health given more companies are now offering these policies. As of 2023, roughly two-thirds of all U.S.-based companies across all industries provided an option for disability insurance, according to data from the Society for Human Resources Management.\par \par \uc2 "These are products you hope you never have to use," said Corey Kines, voluntary benefits sales leader at \uc2 OneDigital\uc2 , a consulting firm. "You may think 'God, it's coming out of my paycheck every month -- am I ever going to use this?' But then if you do have to use them, you're glad that you have them."\par \par \uc2 What disability insurance does\par \par \uc2 Short-term disability insurance protects a portion of employees' income for those who intend to return to work after recovering from an injury or illness.\par \par \uc2 Many short-term disability policies also allow pregnant workers to take maternal benefits after giving birth.\par \par \uc2 Most short-term disability policies provide coverage ranging from three to 12 months, which is determined by state law and the individual insurance.\par \par \uc2 Sometimes, employees have to wait up to three months before their benefits kick in.\par \par \uc2 A handful of states, including California, New York and Rhode Island, require employers to offer short-term disability to workers.\par \par \uc2 Long-term disability insurance pays employees a portion of their income, between 60% and 80% of total salary, beyond the point of short-term disability.\par \par \uc2 Accessing long-term disability benefits often means a longer wait time than for short-term disability.\par \par \uc2 Think of long-term disability insurance as an add-on plan boosting the publicly available coverage, said Jonathan Gruber, professor of economics at the \uc2 Massachusetts Institute of Technology\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Think about paying $20 a month versus you don't pay that and then something does go wrong -- and you have to live the rest of your life on 40% of your wages," he said.\par \par \uc2 What qualifies as a disability?\par \par \uc2 The definition of disability varies by state and depends on the individual policy, Kines said. In most cases, pregnant employees or those struggling with mental health -- two conditions one might not immediately associate with disability -- are able to take advantage of short-term disability to make up their salary while taking time away from the workplace.\par \par \uc2 Employees can scrutinize their policy and ask their HR team to explain what is covered under the offered policy, Gruber said.\par \par \uc2 "You need a responsive and respectful human-resources process that evaluates cases on a consistent basis and can have as bright lines as possible," he said.\par \par \uc2 Just as important as understanding what constitutes disability, Kines said, is defining the term earnings. While some policies protect total income, including commission and other industry-specific pay, other policies only provide base salary, he said.\par \par \uc2 Check with your HR team to best understand what you would receive under this benefit should a disaster strike.\par \par \uc2 Who needs disability insurance?\par \par \uc2 While some employers already incorporate short-term disability insurance into their benefits -- whether by design or by law -- Kines recommends all employees, regardless of age and income, add long-term disability to their plan. The majority of people, he said, would be unable to replace any income lost for the years before retirement.\par \par \uc2 "Behind health insurance, long-term disability insurance is the most important product to have," he said.\par \par \uc2 Younger workers who are new to the workforce might scoff at the prospect of adding another expense to their health-insurance plan, Gruber said. He encourages these "young invincibles" to think twice before skipping ahead.\par \par \uc2 Those nearing retirement age, however, are right to scrutinize the supplemental coverage's overall benefit to their plan, Kines said.\par \par \uc2 A common misconception leads many to believe disability insurance won't protect workers past age 65. But many long-term disability insurance policies will cover at least one additional year, Kines said.\par \par \uc2 "It's a math problem at that point: Is this premium I'm paying worth the potential one year benefit?" he said.\par \par \uc2 Some people can also apply for public disability, which allows people to tap in to their Social Security benefits early.\par \par \uc2 The cost of disability insurance\par \par \uc2 Disability-insurance premiums are calculated according to the individual worker's age, income and industry, among other factors. The cost of the premiums in employer-offered plans is typically between 1% and 3% of the employee's regular income, Brennan said. Some employers offer short-term disability at no cost to their workers.\par \par \uc2 There is often a tax benefit to disability insurance. Workers can pay their premiums with after-tax dollars; that means that come tax time, they won't owe taxes on disability benefits that are paid out.\par \par \uc2 In addition to company-sponsored plans, another option is to get disability coverage from private insurance companies. They can be cheaper but often charge more, health advocates and financial advisers say. Given your employer may be paying part of the premium, there is also a tax benefit to buying through your company.\par \par \uc2 "If it's offered through your employer, it is typically a no-brainer, because it is tax-preferred and it is a risk we all take," Gruber said. "If you're buying it on your own, it's not a no-brainer."\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020231108ejb80000b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
