\par\par \b \uc2 Business News: Carmakers Agree to Pay Striking Workers --- Employees on picket lines are expected to get $100 a day from Detroit's Big Three\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nora Eckert \par \uc2 548 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 November 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B5\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2 , \uc2 General Motors\uc2  and Chrysler parent \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  agreed to pay striking workers for their time on the picket lines, as part of new labor deals reached late last month with the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  to end the union's walkout.\par \par \uc2 The move is an unusual one in the union's history of negotiating with the car companies, according to people familiar with the talks.\par \par \uc2 UAW\uc2  workers who walked the picket lines at the three companies are expected to receive on average slightly more than $100 a day for the time they were out on strike, the people said. This amount is on top of the $500 a week that the \uc2 UAW\uc2  has paid picketing workers out of its own strike fund.\par \par \uc2 More than 45,000 workers in total went on strike over roughly six weeks, although certain locations were out longer than others.\par \par \uc2 Unionized employees at the Detroit automakers who were temporarily laid off as a result of the strike also are expected to receive similar payment from the companies.\par \par \uc2 In recent weeks, the union has reached tentative agreements with each car company, resolving the first simultaneous strike of all three in \uc2 UAW\uc2  history. The deals, if ratified, would raise workers' pay 25% over four years, as well as offer them cost-of-living adjustments and other improved benefits.\par \par \uc2 During a livestream to union members late Thursday, \uc2 UAW\uc2  President Shawn Fain reiterated plans to organize nonunionized factories of foreign automakers such as \uc2 Toyota Motor\uc2 . On Wednesday, \uc2 Toyota\uc2  said it was raising wages by 9% for most U.S. auto factory workers and shortening the time it takes to reach the maximum pay.\par \par \uc2 "\uc2 Toyota\uc2  isn't giving out raises out of the goodness of their heart," Fain said. "They did it now because the company knows we're coming for them."\par \par \uc2 In a statement after Fain's remarks, \uc2 Toyota\uc2  said the decision to unionize ultimately will be made by its workers, and that the company has "a history of stable employment and income for our employees."\par \par \uc2 "By engaging in honest, two-way communication about what's happening in the company, we aim to foster positive morale," \uc2 Toyota\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 Fain also outlined details of the temporary pact the union reached with \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , including the reopening of an assembly plant in Illinois the automaker had indefinitely closed earlier this year.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UAW\uc2  took some concessions in the deal, said Rich Boyer, vice president of the union's \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  department, in the video address. The automaker had sought the right to close and consolidate parts depots and other facilities, such as its North American headquarters, or it would close plants and lead to job losses, the \uc2 UAW\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 "It wasn't an easy choice or decision," Boyer said.\par \par \uc2 Still, Boyer said the \uc2 UAW\uc2  won the elimination of a lower pay scale for workers at the parts-distribution unit, a key demand during this current round of talks.\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  spokeswoman declined to comment. Company officials previously said the proposal for those facilities was intended to modernize its parts-distribution business, not to cut jobs, and that it's not leaving its North American headquarters.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020231103ejb30001s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
89
