\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: As Unions Ride Wave, Tests Await\b0\par\par\uc2 By David Harrison \par \uc2 648 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 November 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2 ' tentative deals with Detroit automakers mark the latest union victory in a year of multiplying strikes and sizable gains in a robust labor market.\par \par \uc2 The agreements could embolden other unions to take aggressive approaches to contract negotiations and fuel more strikes in coming months -- as long as the economy stays strong.\par \par \uc2 If growth cools and unemployment rises, however, as many economists forecast, workers' momentum could chill and tip the balance of power back toward employers.\par \par \uc2 "If there's a recession or if the labor market gets loose again, will that interfere?" said Susan Schurman, a professor of labor studies at \uc2 Rutgers University\uc2 , referring to unions' efforts. "History would predict that it would, but history has not been a good predictor in the last three years."\par \par \uc2 Labor shortages since the pandemic hit gave both unionized and nonunion workers more leverage than they had in decades, driving big boosts in pay and benefits.\par \par \uc2 Union workers, however, have seen slower compensation growth than their nonunion counterparts because it takes time for multiyear union contracts to come up for renewal. Wages and benefits for union workers rose 3.8% in the July-through-September period from a year before, compared with a 4.4% gain for nonunion workers, according to the Labor Department.\par \par \uc2 One factor fueling unions' demands is that they have seen pay rise faster for chief executive officers than for workers since the pandemic, said Kate Bronfenbrenner, a professor at Cornell University's School of Industrial and Labor Relations. In the auto industry, CEOs now make about 300 times what rank-and-file workers make.\par \par \uc2 More than 300,000 union members have walked off the job so far this year, the most since 2019, according to the Labor Department. The U.S. lost more than 11 million workdays to labor disputes this year through September, more than any full year since 2000.\par \par \uc2 Hollywood writers spent five months on strike before winning a new contract in September. In October, 75,000 workers at healthcare giant \uc2 Kaiser Permanente\uc2  won pay increases and higher staffing levels after walking off the job for three days, the industry's largest strike on record.\par \par \uc2 And in August, 330,000 drivers and package sorters at \uc2 United Parcel Service\uc2  represented by the \uc2 International Brotherhood of Teamsters\uc2  ratified a new five-year contract that would bring average pay and benefits for a full-time driver to $170,000. The deal averted a strike.\par \par \uc2 Public support for unions hit 71% last year, the highest level since 1965, before slipping slightly this year, according to Gallup polls. President Biden has been one of their most outspoken backers.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UAW\uc2  strike "raises people's expectations," said John Cakmakci, president of United Food and Commercial Workers Local 951, which represents 28,000 employees at \uc2 Meijer\uc2  grocery stores in Michigan. The union is one of hundreds negotiating a new contract next year.\par \par \uc2 Cakmakci said he hopes to win a 25% to 28% pay raise -- roughly twice the wage increase in the last contract -- as well as more paid time off and more generous health benefits.\par \par \uc2 "There's a bit more confidence in getting it based on what other unions have done," he said.\par \par \uc2 UAW\uc2  President Shawn Fain wants to keep labor's momentum going. The union will now focus on organizing nonunion plants, he said Sunday.\par \par \uc2 But how long the strike wave lasts could depend on broader economic factors, such as the labor market's strength, said Matthew Johnson, an economist at \uc2 Duke University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Many forecasters expect job and wage growth to slow and joblessness to rise over the coming year amid the highest interest rates in more than two decades.\par \par \uc2 "When that labor market cools, it will probably make it so that workers and unions have to temper what they bring to the bargaining table," Johnson said.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw553\pich296\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal8295\pichgoal4440
