\par\par \b \uc2 Union Snags Big Win With New Auto Contracts --- \uc2 GM\uc2  is the last of the Detroit Three to reach tentative deal, ending the strike\b0\par\par\uc2 By Mike Colias \par \uc2 1,089 \uc2 words\par \uc2 31 October 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  struck a tentative labor deal with \uc2 General Motors\uc2  on Monday, ending a six-week strike at the Detroit automakers that delivered the union's most decisive victory for members in decades.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UAW\uc2  secured the proposed pact with \uc2 GM\uc2  after reaching similar deals last week at \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2  and Chrysler-parent \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 . With this latest agreement, the \uc2 UAW\uc2  said it is halting its historic walkout, which sent more than 45,000 workers to picket lines at nine U.S. factories and dozens of parts-distribution centers.\par \par \uc2 That closure is a positive for the carmakers. But it portends difficult times ahead for the Detroit Three with the new pacts expected to push the companies' labor costs higher than initially expected when talks began.\par \par \uc2 "We were relentless in our fight to win a record contract, and that is exactly what we accomplished," \uc2 UAW\uc2  President Shawn Fain said Monday.\par \par \uc2 Ford executives are already talking about the need to offset the higher expenses in this latest deal. The automaker has said the \uc2 UAW\uc2  contract would add $850 to $900 per vehicle in additional costs.\par \par \uc2 "We have work to do," Ford Chief Financial Officer \uc2 John Lawler\uc2  said last week. "We have to identify efficiencies. We have to increase productivity. It is a record contract."\par \par \uc2 The tentative agreements, to be voted on in the coming weeks, include a 25% general wage increase over four years, which with cost-of-living increases would boost the top pay for production workers to about $42 an hour.\par \par \uc2 By the end of the contract's term in 2028, most of the Detroit companies' unionized workers would make in the mid-$80,000s annually, before overtime pay.\par \par \uc2 The return of cost-of-living adjustments -- inflationary protection that gets baked into the hourly wage -- was a nonstarter for the companies when bargaining began. The \uc2 UAW\uc2  gave it up as a concession in 2009 as \uc2 GM\uc2  and Chrysler stumbled toward bankruptcy, but union bargainers got it back into these contracts.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UAW\uc2  scored some other major wins, as well, securing the right to strike over plant closures, higher pay for temporary employees and a shorter, three-year time period in which it takes a production worker to reach the maximum wage.\par \par \uc2 In one of the union's most notable victories, the \uc2 UAW\uc2  secured the reopening of a 1,350-worker factory in Illinois that \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  had idled earlier this year. An adjoining battery facility will also be added to the site, the union said.\par \par \uc2 Auto executives knew heading into talks over the summer that they would need to open their wallets wider than they had in a long time. A tight labor market, high inflation, a run of record profits postpandemic and a revitalized national labor movement had the \uc2 UAW\uc2  primed for major contract wins.\par \par \uc2 Union leaders leaned into that momentum with a hard-charging approach to negotiations and the strike, galvanizing rank-and-file workers who were unhappy with the relatively small contract gains over the past two decades.\par \par \uc2 On Sunday night, Fain called the latest agreements a "turning point" for the union and emphasized that its next stop after Detroit will be trying to organize workers at automakers, such as \uc2 Tesla\uc2 , \uc2 Toyota\uc2  and \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2 , whose U.S. factories aren't unionized.\par \par \uc2 "When we return to the bargaining table in 2028, it won't just be with the Big Three, but with the Big Five or Big Six," Fain said.\par \par \uc2 The tentative deals struck in recent days would be the richest contracts since at least the 1960s, the union said. The wage increases alone over four years total more than workers got in the past 22 years.\par \par \uc2 GM\uc2  Chief Executive \uc2 Mary Barra\uc2  said its agreement with \uc2 UAW\uc2  allowed it to continue investing in its future and provide good U.S. jobs. "We are looking forward to having everyone back to work across all of our operations," she said.\par \par \uc2 A stock-market slide for \uc2 GM\uc2  and Ford has punctuated the uneasiness in Detroit. Ford's shares closed below $10 each for the first time since January 2021, while \uc2 GM\uc2 's stock price hit its lowest mark in nearly seven years, outside of a deep decline after the pandemic hit in 2020.\par \par \uc2 There were other factors that spooked investors beyond the labor uncertainty. Ford badly missed third-quarter earnings expectations, in part because of nagging quality problems. \uc2 GM\uc2 's driverless-car company, Cruise, pulled all of its robot taxis off the road because of regulators' safety concerns. And both companies pulled back on EV-investment plans that investors had cheered.\par \par \uc2 The compounding pieces of bad news over the past week served as vivid reminders that the Detroit companies face challenges to their core car-making business -- such as higher labor costs and pricing pressure -- as they try to pull off the long-term EV transition.\par \par \uc2 For the past five years, \uc2 GM\uc2  and Ford executives have outlined their vision for a rapid shift to electric, digital cars that can be updated similar to an iPhone, and sometimes even take over driving duties. Unlocking new features, they have said, should lead to profitability that someday will surpass the 8% to 10% operating margins they typically generate today.\par \par \uc2 GM\uc2  told investors last week to forget about the company's self-imposed deadlines for hitting EV-production targets over the next year, including a goal of making 400,000 EVs in North America over a two-year stretch ending in mid-2024. Market demand isn't as strong as executives expected, and they want time to re-engineer their EV tech to lower costs.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, Ford said it would delay $12 billion in capital spending on battery plants and other EV projects, saying it doesn't want to get ahead of the market.\par \par \uc2 Those moves will conserve capital spending on a technology that -- for the foreseeable future -- is a money loser.\par \par \uc2 The companies aren't abandoning their electric-transition plans, nor can they afford to. Even if U.S. consumers are fickle, regulators from Washington, D.C., to Germany and Beijing are demanding carbon-emissions cuts from vehicle fleets, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 Tesla\uc2  continues to expand and is more profitable than the Detroit companies, even though waning demand in the U.S. and China has prompted it to cut prices.\par \par \uc2 In addition, increasingly sophisticated Chinese automakers are growing profits, gobbling up market share and expanding fast into Europe.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020231031ejav0001e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
