\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Workers See Growth in Paid Time Off\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jeffrey Sparshott \par \uc2 648 \uc2 words\par \uc2 31 October 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Americans are increasingly getting paid for not doing work.\par \par \uc2 Growth in paid time off -- including family leave, sick leave and vacation -- is widening the gap between the number of hours for which workers get paid and the number of hours they are actually on the job.\par \par \uc2 Employers have expanded paid benefits to retain and attract workers in a hot job market. Employees, meanwhile, are using the new benefits as they juggle work, family and health.\par \par \uc2 Trevor Guy this month used new benefits offered by his restaurant employer to get paid for time he took off from work after the radiator fan on his 2004 Mustang broke on the way to the job.\par \par \uc2 Guy, a 25-year-old manager at Tono Pizzeria + Cheesesteaks, a five-restaurant chain in the Minneapolis-St. Paul area, said he was stuck on the side of the road for six hours and needed a couple more hours to arrange a repair.\par \par \uc2 He called in, the restaurant found someone to cover him, and he received his normal pay while handling one of life's curveballs.\par \par \uc2 "Having that flexibility when life happens is a big benefit," Guy said.\par \par \uc2 Tono's expanded its paid-time-off policy this year to all of its workers, from full-time managers to part-time dishwashers. Full-time staff get 40 hours a year plus an additional hour for every 30 hours on the job. Part-time workers get an hour and a half for every 30 hours.\par \par \uc2 Americans broadly receive less paid leave and work more hours than their peers in other developed countries. Tono's, however, is among many employers expanding such benefits.\par \par \uc2 As of this spring, employers offered 80% of workers paid sick leave, up from 67% a decade ago, according to Labor Department data. Paid vacation expanded to 77% of the workforce from 74%. And paid family leave, with the most dramatic jump, increased to 27% from 12%.\par \par \uc2 During the same period, the unemployment rate dropped to a half-century low of 3.4% from above 7% a decade ago. As of August, there also were about 3.3 million more job openings than jobless people seeking work, according to Labor Department data.\par \par \uc2 As a result, the paid workweek held steady at 34.5 hours over much of the past decade -- except for a surge when businesses raced to gear up as Covid-19 started to fade. The number of hours worked dropped to 32.9 a week in the first half of this year from 33.5 a week a decade ago, according to research from \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of Atlanta\uc2  economists Lei Fang and John Robertson.\par \par \uc2 The rise in paid time off as a part of employee compensation is responsible for the growing gap, the economists concluded.\par \par \uc2 Fewer hours on the job might also reflect changing attitudes toward work, especially since the pandemic. And this appears to be continuing despite cooler job growth this year compared with the prior two years. Separate data from job-search site Indeed shows a steady increase in employers advertising benefits such as paid time off even though job openings have fallen from a recent peak.\par \par \uc2 What was once a perk might have become more broadly ingrained in the labor market as an expected benefit. "I think that's definitely at play here, workers having higher expectations," said Indeed economist Cory Stahle.\par \par \uc2 The balancing act for employers and the economy: Less time on the job could feed into worker shortages that have bedeviled the labor market.\par \par \uc2 "Business owners are already trying to deal with missed sales opportunities because they don't have enough staff," said \uc2 Holly Wade\uc2 , executive director of the research center at the \uc2 National Federation of Independent Business\uc2 , a trade group for small-business owners. "The days off are adding another element of stress."\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw553\pich209\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal8295\pichgoal3135
