\par\par \b \uc2 UAW\uc2 's Widening Of Strike Reflects Strategy --- New walkout at \uc2 GM\uc2  plant when deal was close shows union's embrace of surprise\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nora Eckert, Ryan Felton and Mike Colias \par \uc2 850 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 October 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The decision by the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  to call a fresh strike Saturday at \uc2 General Motors\uc2  as talks appeared to be nearing a conclusion was the latest in a set of unexpected moves by the union, which has used misdirection and maximized publicity to keep companies off balance.\par \par \uc2 The surprise walkout at a factory in Tennessee followed several days of intensifying contract negotiations between \uc2 GM\uc2  and union leaders, including meetings involving \uc2 GM\uc2  Chief Executive \uc2 Mary Barra\uc2  and \uc2 UAW\uc2  President Shawn Fain.\par \par \uc2 The escalation of the strike came as the \uc2 UAW\uc2  confirmed that it reached a tentative agreement with Chrysler-parent \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  on a new labor contract.\par \par \uc2 As workers at \uc2 GM\uc2 's factory in Spring Hill, Tenn., were ordered to go on strike, both sides traded barbs, saying they were disappointed the other party declined to finalize an agreement that was largely similar to the tentative deals struck with \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  and \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2  in the preceding days.\par \par \uc2 About 4,000 workers at the Spring Hill factory assemble three Cadillac SUV models, as well as engines that get shipped to other \uc2 GM\uc2  plants.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UAW\uc2 's tentative agreement with \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  moved the union one step closer to ending a historic strike that has dinged all three automakers and put more than 45,000 workers on the picket lines for six weeks.\par \par \uc2 The terms of the deals at Ford and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  largely mirror one another on core economics.\par \par \uc2 On Sunday night, \uc2 UAW\uc2  leaders rolled out the additional details of the Ford contract during a livestream.\par \par \uc2 Among some of the new specifics are a breakdown of the pay raises, which including cost-of-living increases would boost starting pay from about $18 an hour to $30 an hour and the top wage to about $42 an hour by the end of the agreement in 2028. Skilled trades workers would make about $50 an hour if the new agreement is ratified.\par \par \uc2 Union bargainers additionally secured $8.1 billion in investment commitments for Ford factories over the next four years, ensuring job security for members at those sites.\par \par \uc2 "We went to Ford with a goal of not leaving a dime on the table, and we accomplished that," Fain said Sunday night.\par \par \uc2 The proposed contract also offers a $5,000 ratification bonus, a bump in the 401(k) contribution from 6.4% to 10% for production workers and other perks, such as a $1,500 car voucher toward a new-vehicle purchase.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UAW\uc2  said it won a path to put one battery plant in Michigan and another new electric-vehicle assembly factory in Tennessee under the master \uc2 UAW\uc2  contract if future workers there agree to join the union. Ford paused construction on that battery operation last month. \uc2 UAW\uc2  leaders didn't mention the other battery plants Ford is building through joint-ventures with South Korean battery maker SK On.\par \par \uc2 In executing strikes at all three companies simultaneously, the union broke with historical practice by targeting specific factories at each rather than an all-out walkout at one automaker.\par \par \uc2 The strike began in mid-September and expanded as Fain called additional walkouts.\par \par \uc2 The financial toll of the factory shutdowns is mounting for the companies. \uc2 GM\uc2  last week said the strike is costing it $200 million a week in lost profit.\par \par \uc2 At Ford, the strike is expected to cost it about $1.3 billion in pretax profit this year.\par \par \uc2 Shares of all three companies sold off on Friday. Ford's stock fell 12% following its third-quarter earnings report Thursday evening that fell short of analysts' expectations.\par \par \uc2 GM\uc2  negotiators bargained with the union into the early morning hours on Saturday and were back at the table in the afternoon. The sides were closing in on final terms as of late Friday, people with knowledge of the talks said.\par \par \uc2 If approved by members, the tentative contracts at Ford and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  would mark the union's biggest gains in decades.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, auto executives have warned that a too-rich labor agreement could hurt the companies' financial futures as they spend billions of dollars to convert their lineups to electric vehicles and catch up with \uc2 Tesla\uc2 , whose factories aren't unionized.\par \par \uc2 At Ford, Chief Financial Officer \uc2 John Lawler\uc2  said Thursday during the company's earnings call that the proposed contract could add between $850 to $900 per vehicle in labor costs.\par \par \uc2 Similar to the Ford agreement, the proposed deal with \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  provides more in overall base wage increases than company workers have received in the past two decades. The temporary agreement includes a right to strike over product and investment commitments, a first for the union, the \uc2 UAW\uc2  said, and it reduces the amount of time for workers to reach the top wage by more than half, to three years.\par \par \uc2 In a significant win, the \uc2 UAW\uc2  secured the reopening of an assembly plant in Belvidere, Ill. \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  said in December that it would indefinitely close the 1,350-employee factory.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020231030ejau0001p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
