\par\par \uc2 REVIEW & OUTLOOK (Editorial)\par \b \uc2 Ford Pays Dearly for Labor Peace\b0\par\par\uc2 433 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 October 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 President Biden praised what he called the "historic tentative agreement" that the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  and Ford struck late Wednesday as a testament to collective bargaining. Perhaps, but the real test will be whether Ford can stay competitive, especially amid the government-forced electric-vehicle transition.\par \par \uc2 UAW\uc2  President Shawn Fain's strategy of staging strikes at select plants operated by all three Detroit auto makers appears to be paying off. The union was threatening to call off workers at a factory that builds Ford's highly profitable F-150 pickup when the auto maker agreed to a generous deal that ends the nearly six-week walkout.\par \par \uc2 Ford agreed to raise wages 25% over four years and restore cost-of-living adjustments -- a major improvement on its initial 15% offer. Ford's top pay rate will increase by more than 30% to more than $40 an hour. The starting wage will rise 68% to more than $28 an hour, which is more than a typical college grad makes straight out of school.\par \par \uc2 Ford's initial offer eliminated lower wages for newer hires -- implemented when the Big Three auto makers verged on collapse in 2007 -- but the union also won a faster progression to top pay. The union says members will receive more in general wage increases over the next four-and-a-half years than they have over the last 22 years combined.\par \par \uc2 Ford appears to have made the calculation that the high price is worth labor peace. That's how collective bargaining works, and investors can decide if the deal is worth it. That may take a while to become clear, as Ford has to pump more capital into producing money-losing EVs that governments are mandating.\par \par \uc2 Ford this summer said it expected to lose $4.5 billion this year on EVs, though it could be more since last week it recalled 35,000 Mustang Mach-Es because of a manufacturing defect. The \uc2 UAW\uc2  deal will cost Ford an estimated $900 million in the first year and could make it harder to stay competitive with foreign auto makers and \uc2 Tesla\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Perhaps Ford will rely on employee attrition and automation to hold down costs. Automation has helped make \uc2 Tesla\uc2  profitable, and more of it is inevitable. \uc2 UAW\uc2  members will be the long-term losers if Wednesday's deal causes their jobs to vanish.\par \par \uc2 (See related letter: "Letters to the Editor: The Government-Fueled EV Bubble Will Burst" -- WSJ Nov. 2, 2023)\par \par \uc2 (See related letter: "Letters to the Editor: What Do We Owe the \uc2 UAW\uc2 ?" -- WSJ Nov. 9, 2023)\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020231027ejar0001b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
