\par\par \b \uc2 Ford Posts $1.2 Billion Profit, Withdraws Guidance for Year\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nora Eckert \par \uc2 676 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 October 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2  posted $1.2 billion in net income for the third quarter, swinging from a loss in the prior-year period, but it withdrew its guidance for the year, citing the impact of the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  strike.\par \par \uc2 The company also said it is pushing out $12 billion in planned investment on electric vehicles, reiterating concerns about consumer demand and pricing pressure from competitors. It plans to delay a second battery plant in the U.S., a facility in Kentucky that is a joint venture with Korean battery maker SK On.\par \par \uc2 "Given the dynamic EV environment, we are judicious about our production," said Ford Finance Chief \uc2 John Lawler\uc2  on the company's earnings call.\par \par \uc2 In the quarter, revenue rose 11% from the year-ago period to $43.8 billion, boosted by strong sales of its highly profitable pickup trucks. Its pretax profit, excluding one-time items, was $2.2 billion.\par \par \uc2 Ford recorded a $827 million loss in the third quarter of 2022, largely due to a hefty charge associated with the shutdown of the auto maker's autonomous-driving venture \uc2 Argo\uc2  AI.\par \par \uc2 Adjusted earnings-per-share of 39 cents for the quarter missed analysts' expectations of 46 cents for the July-to-September period. The company's stock fell 4% in after hours trading.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, after months of talks and strikes at three of its factories, Ford struck an tentative labor contract for its 57,000 hourly workers in the U.S., effectively halting the walkout and paving the way for unionized employees to return to work.\par \par \uc2 Ford suspended its pretax profit guidance for 2023, citing the strike and the pending labor guidance, after raising it in July to a range of $11 billion to $12 billion. \uc2 General Motors\uc2  also withdrew its profit outlook earlier this week, referencing the strike, which continues at its factories.\par \par \uc2 The company forecasts the six-week walkout to have cost it about $1.3 billion in adjusted earnings in total over the two quarters, including $100 million in the last three-month period.\par \par \uc2 "We're not going to get out ahead of ourselves," Lawler said, of the ramifications of the proposed labor deal and emphasized the company will be profitable moving forward.\par \par \uc2 "This is an increase in cost for us," he said. "We're going to have to find efficiencies and productivity throughout the system and throughout the company to help mitigate the impacts."\par \par \uc2 The tentative contract, which is subject to member approval, would secure workers a 25% wage increase and the return of cost-of-living adjustments, among other wins for the \uc2 UAW\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Investors will be watching closely on how much this new contract is going to cost Ford and how the company plans to offset higher labor costs in other parts of the business.\par \par \uc2 The contract is expected to add about $1.5 billion to Ford's annual labor costs, equal to roughly 13% of its global operating profit based on the company's most recent earnings guidance, according to \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Company executives have said higher labor costs could hurt its ability to remain competitive, particularly with rivals like \uc2 Tesla\uc2  that don't have unionized workforces, and comes as Ford is investing heavily in its electric-vehicle business.\par \par \uc2 Ford's per-hour labor cost, including benefits, would be around $67 this year and rise to about $88 by the final full year of the contract, in 2027, according to \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2 . That is significantly higher than the labor costs of the foreign automakers, estimated in the mid-$50s per hour, and \uc2 Tesla\uc2  at $45 to $50.\par \par \uc2 Like other car companies, Ford has expressed uneasiness about the EV market and buyers' ability to pay up for the technology as the space becomes more crowded with competitors.\par \par \uc2 Through the first three quarters of this year, Ford has lost $3.1 billion in operating profit on its electric and software division, already surpassing its earlier projection that it would lose $3 billion this year on the unit.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw553\pich450\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal8295\pichgoal6750
