\par\par \b \uc2 UAW\uc2 , Ford Reach Tentative Labor Pact\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nora Eckert \par \uc2 677 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 October 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  secured a tentative labor deal with \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2  Wednesday night, potentially ending a six-week strike at one automaker while negotiations continue at \uc2 General Motors\uc2  and Chrysler-parent \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The labor agreement, which is subject to a member vote before being ratified, contains a 25% wage increase during the span of the contract, including an 11% bump in the first year, according to \uc2 Chuck Browning\uc2 , the \uc2 UAW\uc2 's lead bargainer with Ford. The overall increase, which will be spread out over four years, would put the top wage for assembly workers at around $40 an hour.\par \par \uc2 Workers will also receive cost-of-living adjustments, which were suspended in 2009, and the right to strike over plant closures, Browning said. Terms of the deal additionally cut the time it takes for new hires to reach the top wage, to three years from eight in the contract that expired last month, the \uc2 UAW\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 "We told Ford to pony up and they did," \uc2 UAW\uc2  President Shawn Fain said in a video address Wednesday night. "We won things nobody thought was possible." He added that the value of Ford's offer increased 50% since the strike was called on Sept. 14.\par \par \uc2 Ford Chief Executive \uc2 Jim Farley\uc2  said the company is pleased to have reached a tentative agreement and is focused on getting people back to work.\par \par \uc2 If approved by workers, the deal marks the union's biggest contract gains in decades and a resolution to Ford's first \uc2 UAW\uc2  national strike in more than 40 years. It covers 57,000 \uc2 UAW\uc2 -represented workers at Ford's U.S. operations.\par \par \uc2 Ford's tentative agreement puts pressure on \uc2 GM\uc2  and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  to reach deals. The three companies have historically tried to match terms, particularly on key economic issues such as pay, retirement and health benefits, a process known as pattern bargaining.\par \par \uc2 "It becomes a much more existential fight if they truly try to break the pattern," said Marc Robinson, a consultant who spent more than 30 years at \uc2 GM\uc2 , including on labor strategy.\par \par \uc2 GM\uc2  and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  said they are working with the \uc2 UAW\uc2  to reach a tentative agreement as soon as possible.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UAW\uc2 's Ford council needs to approve its tentative pact with the automaker before it can go to members for a vote. Union leaders have called on striking Ford workers to return to the job while voting is under way.\par \par \uc2 "We're going back to work at Ford to keep the pressure on \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  and \uc2 GM\uc2 ," Browning said in the video. "The last thing they want is for Ford to get back to full capacity while they mess around and lag behind."\par \par \uc2 Ford has described its offer to the union as its richest in history, with record wage increases and improved retirement benefits and terms for temporary workers. Company executives have also warned that significantly higher labor costs would hamper the automaker's ability to compete with rivals that have nonunionized workforces.\par \par \uc2 Since July, the \uc2 UAW\uc2  has been bargaining individually with the three car companies for new labor contracts that would take effect upon worker ratification and run for more than four years. The union represents about 146,000 U.S. factory workers at the three companies.\par \par \uc2 Fain initiated the strike in mid-September with walkouts at one factory for each company, the first time in the union's 88-year history that it went on strike at all three automakers.\par \par \uc2 Since then, it has expanded to include more than 45,000 workers at eight assembly plants and dozens of parts-distribution facilities.\par \par \uc2 The negotiations have been far more contentious than in years past, with \uc2 UAW\uc2  leaders and executives clashing publicly.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UAW\uc2  early on came out with some aggressive demands, including a 40% wage hike over four years for its members that would be its largest increase in recent memory.\par \par \uc2 It has also pressed the return of cost-of-living adjustments to protect against inflation, better retirement benefits and the right to strike over plant closures.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020231026ejaq0001z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
