\par\par \b \uc2 UAW\uc2  Calls Strike at Large GM Factory\b0\par\par\uc2 By Mike Colias \par \uc2 268 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 October 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  expanded its strike against Detroit's automakers with a walkout at one of \uc2 General Motors\uc2 ' largest and most profitable factories, marking the second straight day of escalation by the union.\par \par \uc2 About 5,000 unionized workers walked out of \uc2 GM\uc2 's Arlington, Texas, assembly plant Tuesday morning, the union said. The plant makes several large sport-utility vehicles, including the Chevrolet Suburban and Tahoe, GMC Yukon and Cadillac Escalade, which are \uc2 GM\uc2 's highest-profit-margin vehicles globally.\par \par \uc2 The walkout came within a few hours of \uc2 GM\uc2 's release of third-quarter earnings, which showed net income declined 7% to $3.1 billion. \uc2 UAW\uc2  President Shawn Fain cited \uc2 GM\uc2 's earnings report in announcing the move, and said \uc2 GM\uc2 's latest contract offer doesn't go far enough in sharing the profits with workers. "It's time \uc2 GM\uc2  workers, and the whole working class, get their fair share," Fain said.\par \par \uc2 GM\uc2  called the strike at the Arlington plant "unnecessary and irresponsible." The company said it gave the union an offer last week that sweetened a previous proposal that the automaker had described as a record contract.\par \par \uc2 "It is time for us to finish this process," the company said.\par \par \uc2 The unannounced strike came one day after about 6,800 \uc2 UAW\uc2  workers walked out of a suburban Detroit factory where Chrysler parent \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  makes Ram pickup trucks, among the company's most profitable and highest-selling models.\par \par \uc2 The back-to-back walkouts together mark the sharpest escalation of the union's strike since 