\par\par \b \uc2 GM Scales Back EV Plans as Buyers Balk --- Detroit automaker posts strong profit for quarter but cites a slowing market\b0\par\par\uc2 By Mike Colias \par \uc2 1,038 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 October 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 General Motors\uc2  is abandoning a self-imposed target to build 400,000 electric vehicles by mid-2024, the latest sign that automakers are concerned about the viability of the market for battery-powered cars.\par \par \uc2 The Detroit automaker walked back the goal while reporting a healthy third-quarter profit, despite the hit from the continuing \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  strike. The walkout, which began in mid-September, is now costing GM about $200 million a week in profit.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, the \uc2 UAW\uc2  further expanded the walkouts at GM, targeting a 5,000-worker factory in Texas that makes sport-utility vehicles and is among its most profitable assembly plants.\par \par \uc2 The move on EVs is a surprise one for a company that has bet its future on the technology, anticipating that it will eventually phase out sales of gasoline-powered vehicles next decade. It comes as rivals, including \uc2 Tesla\uc2  and \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2 , also have raised red flags about consumer demand for EVs and buyers' willingness to pay a premium for them over traditional models.\par \par \uc2 In July, \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2  pushed back its EV-output target by one year. In recent months, amid higher interest rates that are making the already pricey cars more expensive for many buyers, EV sales growth has slowed and unsold models are piling up on dealership lots.\par \par \uc2 GM planned to have produced 400,000 EVs over a roughly two-year stretch by the middle of next year but has abandoned that goal. Chief Financial Officer \uc2 Paul Jacobson\uc2  on Tuesday cited a slowdown in the market for battery-powered cars.\par \par \uc2 Last week, GM said it would delay the opening of an EV truck factory in suburban Detroit by a year. Company executives said the automaker stands by its goal of producing one million EVs in North America by the end of 2025 and is trying to build in more flexibility in its manufacturing operations to adjust for fluctuations in demand.\par \par \uc2 Compared with its competitors, GM has been among the most bullish in pursuing a transition to EVs. Investors cheered its declaration in early 2021 that it would phase out nearly all gasoline- and diesel-powered vehicles by 2035, making it among the first automakers to vow a full switch to electric.\par \par \uc2 The question now is whether these pullbacks signal that the car companies wagered too heavily on EVs or are simply confronting hiccups in their transformation toward an electric-dominated future that \uc2 Tesla\uc2  pioneered. GM Chief Executive \uc2 Mary Barra\uc2  on Tuesday said she believed it was the latter.\par \par \uc2 "As we get further into the transformation to EV, it's a bit bumpy, which is not unexpected," Barra told analysts during GM's earnings call. "What we're moving to is something that we can react in a much more agile way to make sure that we have the right vehicles."\par \par \uc2 So far, this shift isn't hitting results, though the strike is. The company's net income for the July-to-September period fell 7% to about $3.1 billion. The strike impact and higher expenses on warranty repairs contributed to the drop, offsetting strong pricing as car shoppers continued turning out despite higher interest rates and a slowing economy.\par \par \uc2 The results beat analyst estimates. Operating profit amounted to $2.28 a share, compared with analysts' average estimate of $1.87, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2 . GM also withdrew its full-year profit guidance because of the strike.\par \par \uc2 EV sales in the U.S. still are growing at a faster clip than the broader auto market, but the pace has slowed, and the prices that automakers are commanding have weakened.\par \par \uc2 Several factors are contributing to the slowdown. As EV sales took off over the past three years, early adopters were willing to pay top dollar for new, buzzed-about models, and average EV prices soared above $60,000.\par \par \uc2 Now, carmakers are having to reduce prices and offer discounts on EVs to stimulate interest, moves that cut into profitability.\par \par \uc2 "We expect the EV market to remain volatile until the winners and losers shake out," Ford Chief Executive \uc2 Jim Farley\uc2  said this summer.\par \par \uc2 EV leader \uc2 Tesla\uc2  has led the industry in price cuts, significantly slashing the cost of its most popular vehicles, the Model Y SUV and Model 3 sedan this year.\par \par \uc2 Traditional carmakers -- many of which are just now rolling out several new electric models -- have followed with reductions of their own, complicating the business case for EVs that already were unprofitable in many cases.\par \par \uc2 Last week, \uc2 Tesla\uc2  Chief Executive \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  was gloomy about the outlook for EVs, pointing to sharply higher interest rates as a factor in consumers' willingness to pay up for the technology. Generally, EVs are priced higher than comparably sized gasoline-powered vehicles.\par \par \uc2 When asked about a planned factory in Mexico, Musk said he was hesitant to "go full tilt" on the project. "I don't want to be going at top speed into uncertainty," he said during the company's earnings call.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, EV startup \uc2 Fisker\uc2  said it would cut prices of its most expensive version of its Ocean SUV, a model that it only recently launched in the U.S. Rival startup \uc2 Lucid Group\uc2  is rolling out a referral program that offers discounts on its high-end Air sedan, a tactic aimed at juicing sales of its debut model.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, Barra said GM still is committed to "an all-electric future." GM will calibrate its EV production based on market demand, company executives said, adding that several factories produce both electric and gasoline-powered cars.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UAW\uc2  strike, now in its sixth week, hurt GM's bottom line by $800 million, about one-quarter of which came in the third quarter, Jacobson said.\par \par \uc2 Outside of that, he said, it was an otherwise strong quarter. GM's average vehicle sold for about $50,750 during the period, down only slightly from last year, despite analysts' expectations for a significant falloff in consumers' ability to spend up on new cars.\par \par \uc2 "The consumer has held up remarkably well," Jacobson said, allowing pricing to hold up better than had been expected heading into 2023.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw553\pich472\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal8295\pichgoal7080
