\par\par \b \uc2 UAW\uc2  Strike Hits Big Stellantis Plant --- Walkout sends 6,800 workers to picket lines at key pickup-truck factory\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ryan Felton \par \uc2 856 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 October 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  on Monday expanded its continuing strike against Detroit's car companies by shutting down a pickup-truck plant, a surprise action that hit Chrysler-parent \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 's largest U.S. factory.\par \par \uc2 The 6,800-worker plant, located in Sterling Heights, Mich., in suburban Detroit, makes the Ram 1500 pickup truck, among the automaker's most profitable vehicles. The walkout brings the total number of \uc2 UAW\uc2 -represented workers on strike at the Detroit automakers, including \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2  and \uc2 General Motors\uc2 , to more than 40,000, the \uc2 UAW\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 "We are outraged that the \uc2 UAW\uc2  has chosen to expand its strike action against \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 ," the company said in a statement Monday.\par \par \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  presented a new offer to the union on Thursday that included a 23% wage increase over the life of the contract and a nearly 50% bump in its contribution to employee retirement plans, along with added job security, the company said. "We left the bargaining table expecting a counterproposal, but have been waiting for one ever since."\par \par \uc2 The decision to target the \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  facility comes days after \uc2 UAW\uc2  President Shawn Fain told members that the car companies have made some progress in meeting the union's demands, but he still believes the \uc2 UAW\uc2  can get more. He said both \uc2 GM\uc2  and Ford also are offering 23% wage increases.\par \par \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , which has the highest revenue and profit among the automakers, lags behind both Ford and \uc2 GM\uc2  in addressing the \uc2 UAW\uc2 's demands, the union said Monday. The global car maker's proposals for cost-of-living adjustments, pay for temporary workers and the number of years it is offering to have workers earn a top wage in a new contract were "the worst proposal on the table," the \uc2 UAW\uc2  said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 The 88-year-old union is in its sixth week of a strike in which for the first time the \uc2 UAW\uc2  has simultaneously targeted all three car companies at once. Rather than call an all-out strike at one company, the \uc2 UAW\uc2  has been gradually expanding the strike at targeted facilities, a strategy aimed at keeping companies off balance while also preserving a fund to pay workers on the picket line.\par \par \uc2 Workers made about 221,500 pickups at the Sterling Heights facility in the first nine months of the year, around 27% of \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 's overall U.S. output, according to data from research firm Wards Intelligence. The company also makes Ram pickups at another suburban Detroit factory as well as at a plant in Mexico.\par \par \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  was formed in January 2021 through the merger of \uc2 Fiat Chrysler Automobiles\uc2  and France's \uc2 PSA Group\uc2 , making it the fourth largest in the world by sales. Unlike Ford and \uc2 GM\uc2 , which generate nearly all of their profits in North America, \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  has a strong presence in South America and established European brands such as \uc2 Peugeot\uc2  and \uc2 Opel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The automaker's operating-profit margin for the North American region in 2022 was 16.4% compared with 10.1% for \uc2 GM\uc2  and 8.4% for Ford.\par \par \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  Chief Executive \uc2 Carlos Tavares\uc2  also has a reputation in the auto industry as a cost-cutter who has turned around money-losing brands in France and Germany, in part by winning concessions from Europe's tough union heads.\par \par \uc2 Ram is one of the company's main profit drivers in North America. At the end of September, Ram had 71 days of unsold supply on or en route to dealership lots, a measure of how much stock is available if the company's plants were immediately shut down, according to data from Motor Intelligence, an industry-research firm.\par \par \uc2 Fain's fiery rhetoric at the \uc2 UAW\uc2  has been a source of consternation among Detroit's top car executives. Last week, Ford Executive Chairman \uc2 Bill Ford\uc2  called for an end to the "acrimonious" round of talks, saying the escalating strike threatens the industry's future. \uc2 GM\uc2  Chief Executive \uc2 Mary Barra\uc2  has accused the \uc2 UAW\uc2  of not having real intent of getting a deal finished.\par \par \uc2 Tavares hasn't spoken on the talks since before the strike began, deferring to his North American team.\par \par \uc2 Fain dismissed the executives' concerns on Friday, saying there would be consequences if the companies don't continue to move closer to the \uc2 UAW\uc2 's demands.\par \par \uc2 The union leader said he wanted the automakers to come up more on wages. Until recently, Ford was the only one of the three companies to have a 23% wage increase on the table, while the other two had been at 20%. The companies began talks in the summer with offers of around 10%.\par \par \uc2 Fain also said he wanted the carmakers to move temporary workers to full-time status faster, while also boosting their matching contributions to employee retiree plans.\par \par \uc2 The companies have agreed to eliminate certain wage tiers that pay workers in some areas at lower rates, and they would cut the time it takes to reach the top wage roughly in half, from the current period of eight years.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020231024ejao0001x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
