\par\par \b \uc2 Strike Hurts Car Sector, Spares Buyers for Now\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sean McLain and Ryan Felton \par \uc2 863 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 October 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Six weeks into \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2 ' strike, the impact of the work stoppages are rippling through the car business, causing pain for the automakers, as well as parts makers and factory workers.\par \par \uc2 One corner of the industry that has been largely spared: dealerships and consumers, due in part to an inventory buildup of cars and parts before the strikes.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, the \uc2 UAW\uc2  expanded the walkouts, this time targeting a 6,800-worker factory in suburban Detroit that makes the Ram 1500 trucks.\par \par \uc2 The plant, owned by Chrysler parent \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , is the automaker's largest in the U.S. and among its most profitable, making highly lucrative pickups.\par \par \uc2 With this latest action, there are more than 40,000 \uc2 UAW\uc2  members on strike at \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , \uc2 General Motors\uc2  and \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2  at seven assembly plants and dozens of parts-distribution centers in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 Assembly-line shutdowns resulted in layoffs at parts makers who make components for vehicles that are no longer being produced and cascaded to nonstriking factories at the Detroit car companies that supply parts and materials to facilities that are on walkout.\par \par \uc2 In all, about 6,600 workers at \uc2 GM\uc2 , Ford and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  have been temporarily laid off as a result of the strike, which targeted factories in Kentucky, Michigan, Ohio and Missouri, according to figures released by the companies.\par \par \uc2 In the supply base, thousands of other workers have been idled at parts makers because of the strike. About 40% of suppliers responding to a trade-group survey said they laid off some employees since the start of the walkouts, according to MEMA, an industry group representing parts makers. That figure is expected to jump by the end of October.\par \par \uc2 The shock to the supply chain could intensify as the strike drags on, causing lasting damage to financially fragile parts makers.\par \par \uc2 Customers could face shortages of popular models and replacement parts, but so far, dealers said the impact has been muted. Having experienced a 40-day strike at \uc2 GM\uc2  in 2019, many retailers this time worked to stockpile parts ahead of factory shutdowns.\par \par \uc2 "At this point, it's a wait and see," said Autumn Heacox, a spokeswoman for the California New Car Dealers Association.\par \par \uc2 The lost vehicle production is starting to add up, especially with the \uc2 UAW\uc2 's latest expansion of the strike to a Ford factory in Kentucky that makes highly profitable F-Series Super Duty pickups.\par \par \uc2 With the idling of that factory, the Detroit Three are now losing about 43,000 vehicles a week in factory output, about 30% less than what they were building over the summer, according to industry-services firm \uc2 Cox Automotive\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The idled factories benefited rivals, such as \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  and \uc2 Toyota Motor\uc2 , which picked up market share in September, said Jonathan Smoke, Cox's chief economist.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UAW\uc2  took an unorthodox approach with this strike, opting to target specific plants at three car companies, rather than an all-out walkout at one automaker. The tactic limited the financial pain for \uc2 UAW\uc2  members, most of whom are still working at full pay, and tempered the blow to automakers -- at least initially.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UAW\uc2  warned the strikes could expand the longer there is no labor deal, a move aimed at gradually ratcheting up the pressure on company bargainers by keeping the automakers' biggest moneymaking plants in the union's crosshairs.\par \par \uc2 Based on the lost production to date, \uc2 GM\uc2  and Ford each lost nearly $770 million in earnings before interest and taxes, according to estimates Friday from \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 . \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  lost an estimated $384 million, the bank's analysts wrote in a research note. Investors will get their first glimpse of the strike's toll on \uc2 GM\uc2  and Ford when the two report third-quarter earnings this week.\par \par \uc2 Many suppliers were in a fragile state leading into the strike, after being squeezed in recent years by rising labor and raw-material costs. The pressure is most severe among smaller companies and some are in distress, supply-chain consultants said.\par \par \uc2 The strike at Ford has resulted in more than 13,000 layoffs at nearly 90 supplier sites, said \uc2 Liz Door\uc2 , the company's supply-chain chief.\par \par \uc2 Most suppliers weathered the strike because cash has continued to flow in from prior work, which tends to be paid a month-and-a-half or two months after the shipment was sent.\par \par \uc2 At dealerships, consumers have plenty to pick from when it comes to the domestic brands. All three car companies had enough unsold stock to last them nearly two months or more at the end of September.\par \par \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , which owns \uc2 Jeep\uc2 , Ram, Chrysler and other U.S.-sold brands, had the most supply on dealer lots at the end of September, followed by Ford and then \uc2 GM\uc2 , according to Motor Intelligence, an industry-research firm.\par \par \uc2 The Ram brand had ample stock of its 1500 pickups, enough to last it more than two months, and it builds the pickup at another suburban Detroit factory and a facility in Mexico.\par \par \uc2 Parts continue to trickle into dealership service centers and getting them hasn't become a problem yet, dealers said.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw380\pich515\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal5700\pichgoal7725
