\par\par \b \uc2 Business News: Actors and Studios to Restart Talks\b0\par\par\uc2 By Joe Flint \par \uc2 308 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 October 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Hollywood actors union and major studios and streamers said Saturday they are returning to the negotiating table in an effort to end a monthslong strike that has brought TV and film production to a halt.\par \par \uc2 Talks are scheduled to restart Tuesday, nearly two weeks after the \uc2 Alliance of Motion Picture and Television Producers\uc2  -- which negotiates on behalf of companies such as \uc2 Warner Bros. Discovery\uc2 , \uc2 Walt Disney\uc2  and \uc2 Netflix\uc2  -- walked away from the table because of a revised union demand to share in streaming revenue.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Screen Actors Guild-American Federation of Television and Radio Artists\uc2  is facing pressure from some of its members, including high-profile talent such as George Clooney and Emma Stone, to strike a deal and get the industry back to work. The Writers Guild of America ended its nearly five-month strike against the \uc2 AMPTP\uc2  in late September.\par \par \uc2 The planned negotiations are expected to include senior leadership from entertainment industry giants, the \uc2 AMPTP\uc2  and \uc2 SAG-Aftra\uc2  said in a joint statement.\par \par \uc2 Executives who have been active in negotiations include \uc2 Disney\uc2  Chief Executive \uc2 Bob Iger\uc2 , \uc2 Netflix Co\uc2 -Chief Executive \uc2 Ted Sarandos\uc2 , NBCUniversal Studio Group head \uc2 Donna Langley\uc2  and \uc2 Warner Bros. Discovery\uc2  Chief \uc2 David Zaslav\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Several major stars led by Clooney last week offered to pay more in dues to the union and restructure how royalties are distributed so that less successful performers are compensated first.\par \par \uc2 SAG\uc2  President Fran Drescher said in an \uc2 Instagram\uc2  video that while those offers were generous, it wouldn't pass muster with regulators nor solve the long-term issues that performers are facing.\par \par \uc2 The strikes have wreaked havoc on movie and television production schedules and caused significant economic harm to Los Angeles and other parts of the country that are reliant on the entertainment industry for jobs and revenue.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020231023ejan0001q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
