\par\par \b \uc2 EXCHANGE --- Automakers Avoid More Walkouts -- For Now\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ryan Felton \par \uc2 539 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 October 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2 ' leader said negotiations for a new labor contract with Detroit's three car companies have been progressing, but he threatened to expand the union's now six-week-long strike if they don't move more.\par \par \uc2 UAW\uc2  President Shawn Fain said during a livestream to members Friday that \uc2 General Motors\uc2 , \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2  and Chrysler-parent \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  have each offered 23% wage increases for factory workers over more than four years. That would amount to record contracts for those employees, but the companies still have room to sweeten their offers, he said.\par \par \uc2 "These extremely profitable companies have more to give," Fain said.\par \par \uc2 The 88-year-old union is striking all three Detroit carmakers simultaneously for the first time in its history. About one-in-five of the companies' 146,000 union workers are on strike across six factories and dozens of parts-distribution centers.\par \par \uc2 Fain said Friday that he has heard calls from some members to put a proposed agreement to a vote. He said workers have the ultimate authority, but the union's leadership won't bring a temporary deal for consideration yet.\par \par \uc2 "There is more to be won," Fain said.\par \par \uc2 Until recently, Ford was the only one of the three companies to have a 23% wage increase on the table, while the other two had been at 20%. The companies began talks in the summer with offers of around 10%.\par \par \uc2 Earlier Friday, \uc2 GM\uc2  said its latest offer reflected "substantial movement" in all key areas, adding that most of its factory workers would be making $40.39 an hour, or $84,000 a year by the end of the contract, under that proposal.\par \par \uc2 "The offer on the table is the most significant that \uc2 GM\uc2  has ever proposed to the \uc2 UAW\uc2 ," \uc2 GM\uc2  said earlier Friday. "It is time for us to finish this process."\par \par \uc2 Fain said each of the companies has agreed to eliminate certain wage tiers that pay workers in some areas at lower rates, and they would cut the time it takes to reach the top wage roughly in half, from the current time frame of eight years.\par \par \uc2 The union leader said he wants the companies to come up more on wages, and move temporary workers to full-time status faster. He also noted that each agreed to boost their matching contributions to employee retirement plans, but said they can offer more.\par \par \uc2 Fain also signaled the possibility of future walkouts at some of the companies' most-profitable factories, including plants that build large pickup trucks and sport-utility vehicles.\par \par \uc2 "If they don't move, there will be serious consequences," Fain said.\par \par \uc2 Since workers first walked off the job in mid-September, the strike has scuttled planned production of about 145,000 vehicles, \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  estimated in a research note Friday. That includes ripple effects that have halted production at nonstriking plants.\par \par \uc2 The bank estimates that the strike has cost \uc2 GM\uc2  and Ford around $766 million each in operating profit, and pegs the hit to \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 ' bottom line at around $384 million. \uc2 GM\uc2 , which reports third-quarter earnings, next week, said it lost about $200 million in the three-month period that ended Sept. 30.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020231021ejal0002d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
