\par\par \b \uc2 Business News: Ford Boss Fears Strike Fallout\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nora Eckert \par \uc2 614 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 October 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2  Executive Chair \uc2 Bill Ford\uc2  called for a resolution to an "acrimonious" round of talks with the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  and warned that a continuing strike could hurt the company's ability to keep factory jobs in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 In his first public remarks on the negotiations since they began this summer, the great-grandson of company founder \uc2 Henry Ford\uc2  said the strike and escalating contract battle with the union are threatening the industry's future.\par \par \uc2 "We are at a crossroads," Ford said during a speech at the company's historic Rouge Factory complex, near its headquarters in Dearborn, Mich. "Choosing the right path is not just about Ford's future and our ability to compete. This is about the future of the American automobile industry."\par \par \uc2 Last week, the \uc2 UAW\uc2  extended its strike to include Ford's 8,700-employee truck factory in Louisville, Ky. The plant, which makes Super Duty pickups and Ford Expedition large SUVs, is one of the largest factories in the U.S. and among Ford's most profitable.\par \par \uc2 In his remarks, Ford warned that the fallout from the work stoppage at the Kentucky plant would affect tens of thousands of workers at the company and across its supply chain, and hurt local economies.\par \par \uc2 The union is striking all three Detroit automakers -- Ford, \uc2 General Motors\uc2  and Chrysler-parent \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  -- for the first time in its 88-year history. More than 33,000 of the companies' 146,000 union workers are on strike across six factories and dozens of parts-distribution centers.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, \uc2 UAW\uc2  President Shawn Fain in a livestream to union members singled out Ford Chief Executive \uc2 Jim Farley\uc2 , saying he should "look in the mirror, and look in Ford's bank account."\par \par \uc2 In Ford's speech on Monday, he said the union's contract demands could hurt the automaker's ability to invest in U.S. manufacturing. "If we lose it, we will lose to the competition," he said. "America loses. Many jobs will be lost. So will future investments. We will lose factories."\par \par \uc2 This is the first nationwide \uc2 UAW\uc2  strike at Ford since 1976. The Kentucky truck factory was the third Ford assembly plant where workers walked off, along with an SUV factory in Chicago and another in suburban Detroit. \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2  analysts estimate the strike has cost the automaker $517 million.\par \par \uc2 Sticking points in the talks have included the size of wage increases and retirement benefits.\par \par \uc2 The union also is pressing the companies to include new battery plants under the union's master agreement, worried about job losses as the industry moves to electric vehicles. The automakers have resisted the step because those factories, most of which are still under construction, are separate legal entities and jointly owned by Asian battery manufacturers. Fain said \uc2 GM\uc2  has agreed to include battery production in its \uc2 UAW\uc2  contract, although the company hasn't commented on the issue.\par \par \uc2 Ford, who has been involved in every \uc2 UAW\uc2  negotiation since 1982, said the worsening fight with the \uc2 UAW\uc2  will benefit nonunion competitors, such as foreign automakers and electric-vehicle leader \uc2 Tesla\uc2 . Auto executives have cited those companies' lower labor costs.\par \par \uc2 "This should not be Ford versus the \uc2 UAW\uc2 ," said Ford, flanked by vintage Ford models inside the factory's visitor center. "It should be Ford and the \uc2 UAW\uc2  versus \uc2 Toyota\uc2 , \uc2 Honda\uc2 , \uc2 Tesla\uc2 , and all the Chinese companies that want to enter our home market."\par \par \uc2 The executive chairman said the latest proposal the automaker presented to the union is fair. The company has offered a 23% wage increase over four years, the return of cost-of-living adjustments and other enhancements.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020231017ejah0001n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
