\par\par \b \uc2 EXCHANGE --- Business News: UAW Spares Carmakers More Strikes --- Union president says labor dispute is entering new phase, needs new approach\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nora Eckert \par \uc2 572 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 October 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B9\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  spared \uc2 General Motors\uc2  and Chrysler-parent \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  from a strike escalation Friday, two days after turning up the heat on \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2  with a surprise walkout that targeted one of its most profitable plants.\par \par \uc2 "We're entering a new phase of this fight, and it demands a new approach," \uc2 UAW\uc2  President Shawn Fain said in a livestream Friday. "We are prepared at any time to call on more locals to stand up and walk out."\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UAW\uc2  has sought to keep the pressure on all three automakers during bargaining with a strike strategy that targets specific facilities at each one depending on what movement, or lack thereof, happens at the negotiating table each week.\par \par \uc2 The approach is intended to sow confusion and disrupt the automakers' operations, but with fewer members hitting the picket lines than a traditional all-out strike, a move that helps preserve the union's strike fund.\par \par \uc2 For the past month, Fain has typically announced additional walkouts during Friday live-streams, a pattern that he is now seeking to disrupt in an effort to keep the car companies on edge. Fain specifically called out Ford as growing accustomed to the weekly updates and accused it of slow-walking the union with offers that aren't substantially different.\par \par \uc2 "They stopped being interested in reaching a fair deal now and only became interested in gaming our system of announcing strike expansions on Friday," Fain said.\par \par \uc2 The union initially walked out at three assembly plants for each automaker in mid-September and has since escalated the work stoppage unevenly among the Detroit Three, taking action at parts-distribution facilities as well as additional factories.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, the union ordered 8,700 workers to join the picket lines at a Ford pickup-truck factory in Kentucky that builds some of the company's most profitable vehicles.\par \par \uc2 The move came after talks between Ford and the \uc2 UAW\uc2  during the week hit a standstill after a flurry of activity.\par \par \uc2 In all, more than 33,000 \uc2 UAW\uc2  workers are out on strike, about one-fifth of the union's roughly 146,000 U.S. factory workers at \uc2 GM\uc2 , Ford and \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Ford executives said Thursday the automaker was at the limit of what it could offer to the union. The latest proposal from the automaker included a 23% pay increase over the next four years, the return of cost-of-living adjustments, and a faster timeline to convert temporary workers to full-time.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UAW\uc2  had initially pressed for a 40% wage increase in the next four-year contract but has since bent on that demand, proposing an increase in the mid-30% range.\par \par \uc2 Full-time unionized auto-factory workers currently start off making around $18 an hour and, over eight years, move to a top wage of around $32 an hour. The \uc2 UAW\uc2  is also pushing to narrow the time period required to reach the top wage.\par \par \uc2 Company executives said they had been bargaining around core economic issues, such as retirement benefits, as well as the future of Ford's electric-vehicle battery plants.\par \par \uc2 The battery facilities have become a core discussion topic after the union said last week that \uc2 GM\uc2  agreed to place battery production under its master agreement. \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  and Ford haven't made similar commitments.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020231014ejae00018\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
