\par\par \b \uc2 Business News: Auto Workers Add Ford's Biggest Factory to Strike\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nora Eckert \par \uc2 335 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 October 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 United Auto Workers union\uc2  went on strike at a \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2  pickup-truck plant in Kentucky Wednesday evening, escalating its nearly four-week labor action by hitting the automaker's largest factory.\par \par \uc2 The plant's 8,700 workers walked off the job at 6:30 p.m., marking the largest strike action taken by the \uc2 UAW\uc2  at any of the three Detroit automakers since the union declared a strike against Ford, \uc2 General Motors\uc2  and Chrysler parent \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  in mid-September.\par \par \uc2 The Louisville, Ky., factory makes Super Duty pickup trucks and the Ford Expedition and Lincoln Navigator large SUVs, among the company's biggest moneymakers.\par \par \uc2 "It's time for a fair contract at Ford and the rest of the Big Three," \uc2 UAW\uc2  President Shawn Fain said. "If they can't understand that after four weeks, the 8,700 workers shutting down this extremely profitable plant will help them understand it."\par \par \uc2 Ford said: "The decision by the \uc2 UAW\uc2  to call a strike at Ford's Kentucky Truck Plant is grossly irresponsible but unsurprising given the union leadership's stated strategy."\par \par \uc2 Ford said a work stoppage at the plant would have significant consequences for the company, its workers and suppliers, potentially leading to ripple-effect layoffs at other facilities. The vehicles produced at the plant generate $25 billion a year in revenue, the company said.\par \par \uc2 The strike at the Kentucky factory leaves more than 33,000 of the Detroit automakers' 146,000 unionized workers in the U.S. on strike across six assembly plants and dozens of parts-distribution centers.\par \par \uc2 Ford faces walkouts at an assembly plant near Detroit, which produces the Bronco SUV and Ranger pickup, as well as its Chicago factory, where workers build the Explorer and Lincoln Aviator.\par \par \uc2 Sticking points in the talks, which have been going on since July, include wage hikes, retirement benefits and the status of nearly a dozen future battery factories under development by each company.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020231012ejac0000m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
