\par\par \b \uc2 Deal Ends Strike at GM's Canada Plants\b0\par\par\uc2 By Vipal Monga \par \uc2 596 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 October 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TORONTO -- \uc2 General Motors Canada\uc2  reached a tentative agreement with Canadian auto workers on Tuesday, ending a strike that lasted 13 hours.\par \par \uc2 The three-year agreement covers almost 4,300 workers and includes hourly wage increases ranging from 20% to 25% as well as increases in \uc2 General Motors\uc2 ' contributions to company defined-contribution pension plans.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Unifor union\uc2  had said just after midnight Tuesday that its workers were walking off the job at a Chevrolet Silverado pickup-truck assembly plant, an engine factory and a parts-distribution center in Canada.\par \par \uc2 "This record agreement, subject to member ratification, recognizes the many contributions of our represented team members with significant increases in wages, benefits and job security while building on GM's historic investments in Canadian manufacturing," GM said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 The union said employees returned to work at the three idled plants on Tuesday afternoon.\par \par \uc2 "This is an agreement that is going to change people's lives," said \uc2 Unifor\uc2  President Lana Payne. The union had leverage because GM's Canadian plants are key to the company's U.S. production, she said.\par \par \uc2 A sustained work stoppage in Canada would have further pressured GM, which is grappling with a \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  strike in the U.S. that has stretched into its fourth week.\par \par \uc2 Unifor\uc2 's short-lived strike threatened to pinch supplies of GM's top-selling vehicle, the Silverado, which also is made in the U.S. and Mexico. The walkouts also could have disrupted GM's U.S. production, because engines made at a factory near Niagara Falls are used in the Cadillac Escalade and other highly profitable sport-utility vehicles made in Arlington, Texas.\par \par \uc2 About 25,000 \uc2 UAW\uc2  workers are on strike across five factories and 38 parts-distribution centers owned by Detroit's three unionized automakers: GM, \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2  and Chrysler maker \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 . The union and companies have been bargaining since July for new four-year contracts. Sticking points include wage increases and retirement benefits.\par \par \uc2 Under the tentative agreement with \uc2 Unifor\uc2 , GM will convert its full-time temporary workers to full-time workers, and raise their hourly rates by 28% over the term of the contract. It also will allow workers to reach the highest pay scale in four years, compared with eight years now.\par \par \uc2 Full-time workers also will receive lump-sum bonuses equal to $7,350, and GM will convert the company defined-contribution pension plan to a defined-benefits plan on Jan. 1, 2025.\par \par \uc2 Unifor\uc2  has been emphasizing pension improvements in its talks.\par \par \uc2 Last month, \uc2 Unifor\uc2  members voted to ratify an agreement with Ford, which secured a 15% wage increase and improved pensions. Workers are expected to receive a 10% bump in the first year, plus cost-of-living adjustments, \uc2 Unifor\uc2  said, calling it the largest negotiated wage increase in the union's history.\par \par \uc2 Support for the Ford agreement was lukewarm, however, with only 54% of \uc2 Unifor\uc2  members voting in favor.\par \par \uc2 If \uc2 Unifor\uc2  members ratify the GM pact, the union will start negotiations with \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , which employs 9,500 Unifor members, the most of the three carmakers.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  talks could be more difficult than earlier rounds, however. David Cassidy, president of the local chapter that represents 4,800 workers at an assembly plant in Windsor, Ontario, has said the Ford deal was not good enough for his members.\par \par \uc2 Cassidy also said he wants workers at a new electric-vehicle battery plant in Windsor to be included in the next collective-bargaining agreement.\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  spokeswoman declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020231011ejab00040\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
