\par\par \b \uc2 UAW\uc2  Leaders Trash-Talk Carmakers' Proposals -- Literally --- Wastebasket mania hits auto workers after union leadership adopts a mundane prop\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nora Eckert \par \uc2 947 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 October 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A plain black trash can, the kind that comes straight out of a Staples catalog, has emerged as the unlikely new power symbol of the \uc2 United Auto Workers union\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 UAW\uc2  members have rallied around the waste bin as the union enters its fourth week of strikes at \uc2 General Motors\uc2 , \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2  and Chrysler-parent \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 . The two sides are trying to secure new labor contracts for about 146,000 unionized auto-factory workers.\par \par \uc2 The garbage-can mania began after \uc2 UAW\uc2  President Shawn Fain, a 54-year-old with buzz-cut gray hair and a typically calm demeanor, literally and figuratively trashed what he called a "lowball" proposal from \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In a video update to union members in August, Fain pulled out a rectangular-shaped garbage bin from under his desk and slammed the document into it with an audible clunk.\par \par \uc2 "That's where it belongs," he said, growing visibly agitated. "The trash."\par \par \uc2 It's become the trash-can toss heard around Detroit. Union members have started wearing T-shirts with Fain's face floating above a waste bin stuffed with proposals from the automakers, and there's been broader talk of creating trash-can \uc2 UAW\uc2  merchandise.\par \par \uc2 "For me, it was a turning point," said Ben Bell, who works at a Michigan factory owned by \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 . Before that moment, he said, he was concerned the newly elected Fain wouldn't take a strong enough stance against the car companies. "He was an unproven commodity," Bell said.\par \par \uc2 Ryder Littlejohn, a team leader at a Ford stamping plant in New York, said the action spoke to auto workers' anger and exasperation with the concessions workers have made in past rounds of talks.\par \par \uc2 When Littlejohn saw Fain turn to the unlikely prop, he said, "I literally jumped out of my chair."\par \par \uc2 Littlejohn recruited a co-worker at his factory to fashion an aluminum trash can with \uc2 UAW\uc2  etched on the front, which they mailed to Fain.\par \par \uc2 A few weeks after the first trashing, the \uc2 UAW\uc2  leader yet again pulled out his black, plastic can -- this time with a bargaining proposal from Ford in hand. Viewers littered the comments section in the livestream with trash can emojis.\par \par \uc2 "Straight in the garbage," said one viewer on \uc2 YouTube\uc2 . "File it where it needs to go," said another.\par \par \uc2 Fain said there wasn't enough room in the can. "This trash can is overflowing with the bullsh -- that the Big Three continue to peddle," he said, setting it down.\par \par \uc2 The automakers have not been amused.\par \par \uc2 An executive at \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  wrote in a note to employees after its offer was canned: "The theatrics and personal insults will not help us reach an agreement."\par \par \uc2 The CEOs of Ford and \uc2 GM\uc2  have said Fain is more concerned with attracting media attention than reaching a deal. Executives at the companies have also criticized the union's messaging tactics, emphasizing that the strike has had serious consequences, including the temporary layoffs of thousands of workers at the automakers and suppliers.\par \par \uc2 The companies have put forth offers that they say are already their most lucrative for members in history, but the union has held steadfast to getting more.\par \par \uc2 "The one thing that has really captured the current moment, negotiating with the Big Three, has been the trash can," said Rafael Jaime, president of a \uc2 UAW\uc2  local in California.\par \par \uc2 To tap in to the excitement, he decided to forgo the more traditional union jackets, pins and other trinkets as a reward for a $100 donation to the union's strike fund and instead dole out trash cans like the one Fain uses.\par \par \uc2 Fain, a former electrician from Kokomo, Ind., has proven himself a fiery leader with a strike strategy that has kept the companies guessing. He also has a theatrical bent. He quotes Malcolm X during strike rallies and posts provocative memes riffing off movie clips and "The Bachelorette" online.\par \par \uc2 The idea to trash the proposal struck Fain only a few minutes before he went on camera. He acknowledged that the dramatic gesture may have seemed out of character to those who don't know him well.\par \par \uc2 "This is my typical demeanor," he said quietly in an interview, sitting at a table at the union's headquarters in Detroit, a building known to members as Solidarity House. His mood shifts when he starts thinking about the \uc2 UAW\uc2  members and the sacrifices they've made over the years, he said. "It fires me up."\par \par \uc2 Previous \uc2 UAW\uc2  presidents have occasionally used props in bargaining. The late Stephen Yokich, who led the union in the 1990s, showed up to negotiations with Ford executives with a pair of baseball bats to show how the union was going to go to bat for members.\par \par \uc2 A large, inflatable rat nicknamed Scabby has become a familiar sight at establishments where workers are on strike, intended to warn so-called scab workers from crossing the picket line.\par \par \uc2 Now, \uc2 UAW\uc2 's vigorous use of social media has helped spread the trash-can frenzy.\par \par \uc2 "Massive spike in use of [trash can] emoji among auto workers across America," union communications director Jonah Furman posted in early September on social-media platform X, formerly known as \uc2 Twitter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The black bin has been noticeably absent from Fain's more recent updates on negotiations. It was last visible in the backdrop of an early September livestream, stuffed with crumpled papers and bearing a stark red label: "BIG THREE PROPOSALS."\par \par \uc2 Bell, for one, is ready for it to reappear in Fain's videos. "Every time he touches the trash can, my heart skips a beat," he said.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020231010ejaa00012\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
