\par\par \b \uc2 Business News: \uc2 UAW\uc2  Members Go On Strike At \uc2 Volvo\uc2 's Mack Trucks Unit\b0\par\par\uc2 By Bob Tita \par \uc2 447 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 October 2023\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Nearly 4,000 members of the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  went on strike at \uc2 Volvo Group\uc2 's \uc2 Mack Trucks\uc2  unit after rejecting a five-year contract proposal.\par \par \uc2 UAW\uc2 -represented workers at Mack plants in Pennsylvania, Maryland and Florida walked off their jobs after 73% voted down a contract offer on Sunday. The tentative agreement reached a week earlier between the union's bargaining team and the company included 19% raises over the life of the contract, starting with a 10% increase in the first year and a $3,500 lump-sum bonus for ratifying the contract.\par \par \uc2 "I'm inspired to see \uc2 UAW\uc2  members at \uc2 Mack Trucks\uc2  holding out for a better deal, and ready to stand up and walk off the job to win it," said \uc2 UAW\uc2  President Shawn Fain.\par \par \uc2 Mack President \uc2 Stephen Roy\uc2  said the company was surprised and disappointed that the union went on strike, calling the move unnecessary. "We clearly demonstrated our commitment to good-faith bargaining by arriving at a tentative agreement," he said.\par \par \uc2 The tentative agreement between the company and the union bargaining team allowed for the contract offer to be put to a vote by the union's members.\par \par \uc2 Mack produces medium- and heavy-duty trucks, particularly for trash collection and construction duties. Sweden's \uc2 Volvo Group\uc2  also manufactures \uc2 Volvo\uc2 -brand commercial trucks and construction equipment in the U.S., with those operations based in Greensboro, N.C.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UAW\uc2 's previous four-year contract with the company expired on Oct. 1. The union said it plans to push for higher pay increases, improved cost-of-living allowances, upgraded job security and better pension benefits.\par \par \uc2 About 25,000 \uc2 UAW\uc2  members at Detroit's automakers -- \uc2 General Motors\uc2 , \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2  and Chrysler parent \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  -- remain on strike as the union negotiates new four-year labor agreements. Walkouts began in mid-September and have expanded to include five assembly plants and dozens of parts-distribution centers.\par \par \uc2 Wage increases and cost-of-living adjustments also have been at issue during the talks in Detroit. The companies have proposed increases of about 20% over four years, Fain said Friday, while the union had been pressing for a 40% bump. Retirement benefits and job security also have been priorities of union leaders.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, the union held off on calling further walkouts at the automakers' plants, citing progress in the talks. Fain said \uc2 GM\uc2  agreed to include battery-production work in the \uc2 UAW\uc2 's national bargaining agreement, which he said would help maintain higher levels of pay at new battery factories popping up across the South and Midwest.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Mike Colias contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020231010ejaa0001j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
